 radicals are treated the same as parentheses\n\n- radicals themselves are treated as exponents\n\nTherefore, following the order of operations, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{-125}-\\sqrt{32÷4+8} & = \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{-125}-\\sqrt{8+8} & = \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{-125}-\\sqrt{16} & = \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{(-5)\\cdot (-5)\\cdot (-5)}-\\sqrt{4\\cdot 4} & = \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{(-5)^{3}}-\\sqrt{{4}^{2}} & = \\\\ -5-4 & = \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-dff208c3-d474-5724-99f4-5ec2d8536af2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-dff208c3-d474-5724-99f4-5ec2d8536af2" :answer-field/id #uuid "dff208c3-d474-5724-99f4-5ec2d8536af2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-c005c49e-d357-58f6-9b76-75b38741ed15" :answer-field/choices ["answer-1fc96d39-b4df-5df5-8379-f35ba11363d7" "answer-c37898ce-b678-5d13-b8f9-0f008b58d4d1" "answer-c005c49e-d357-58f6-9b76-75b38741ed15" "answer-b822cb1e-6b10-5fad-8cb3-d36da8524f63" "answer-db59c490-c630-5a09-9a65-b19d31491fab"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1fc96d39-b4df-5df5-8379-f35ba11363d7" :answer/id #uuid "1fc96d39-b4df-5df5-8379-f35ba11363d7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c37898ce-b678-5d13-b8f9-0f008b58d4d1" :answer/id #uuid "c37898ce-b678-5d13-b8f9-0f008b58d4d1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c005c49e-d357-58f6-9b76-75b38741ed15" :answer/id #uuid "c005c49e-d357-58f6-9b76-75b38741ed15" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-9" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b822cb1e-6b10-5fad-8cb3-d36da8524f63" :answer/id #uuid "b822cb1e-6b10-5fad-8cb3-d36da8524f63" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-db59c490-c630-5a09-9a65-b19d31491fab" :answer/id #uuid "db59c490-c630-5a09-9a65-b19d31491fab" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-50acaf8f-e65d-537d-be92-5374b0715e0a" :question/id #uuid "50acaf8f-e65d-537d-be92-5374b0715e0a" :question/math-academy-id "q-99407" :question/problem "Find the value of  $(\\sqrt{36}+16)÷(-7-4).$" :question/worked-solution "To evaluate the given expression, we use PEMDAS. In terms of the order of operations: \n\n- expressions underneath radicals are treated the same as parentheses\n\n- radicals themselves are treated as exponents\n\nTherefore, following the order of operations, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\sqrt{36}+16)÷(-7-4) & = \\\\ (\\sqrt{36}+16)÷(-11) & = \\\\ (\\sqrt{6\\cdot 6}+16)÷(-11) & = \\\\ (\\sqrt{{6}^{2}}+16)÷(-11) & = \\\\ (6+16)÷(-11) & = \\\\ 22÷(-11) & = \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-1116abcc-56e2-5d6f-a608-53f2c148f2a8"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-1116abcc-56e2-5d6f-a608-53f2c148f2a8" :answer-field/id #uuid "1116abcc-56e2-5d6f-a608-53f2c148f2a8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-edde2d09-ca70-53d3-8e63-3242c380bf5d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-edde2d09-ca70-53d3-8e63-3242c380bf5d" "answer-1bf2b462-2502-592a-b0ce-07bd8bf7b790" "answer-b6e64d39-c835-59ae-a49d-efc231137210" "answer-44ce22a6-0172-53a7-b33b-5c382ed5ecf2" "answer-e36757c9-fa2f-5360-aa90-a67c5ef3c9ba"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-edde2d09-ca70-53d3-8e63-3242c380bf5d" :answer/id #uuid "edde2d09-ca70-53d3-8e63-3242c380bf5d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1bf2b462-2502-592a-b0ce-07bd8bf7b790" :answer/id #uuid "1bf2b462-2502-592a-b0ce-07bd8bf7b790" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-22" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b6e64d39-c835-59ae-a49d-efc231137210" :answer/id #uuid "b6e64d39-c835-59ae-a49d-efc231137210" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-44ce22a6-0172-53a7-b33b-5c382ed5ecf2" :answer/id #uuid "44ce22a6-0172-53a7-b33b-5c382ed5ecf2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "22" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e36757c9-fa2f-5360-aa90-a67c5ef3c9ba" :answer/id #uuid "e36757c9-fa2f-5360-aa90-a67c5ef3c9ba" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186078452 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-22c774c3-0c22-5336-81d9-8b5ada9d82d8"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-22c774c3-0c22-5336-81d9-8b5ada9d82d8" :question/id #uuid "22c774c3-0c22-5336-81d9-8b5ada9d82d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-136199" :question/problem "$\\sqrt{6}÷(2-{3}^{2})=$" :question/worked-solution "To simplify the given expression, we use PEMDAS. In terms of the order of operations: \n\n- expressions underneath radicals are treated the same as parentheses\n\n- radicals themselves are treated as exponents\n\nTherefore, following the order of operations, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{6}÷(2-{3}^{2}) & = \\\\ \\sqrt{6}÷(2-9) & = \\\\ \\sqrt{6}÷(-7) & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{(-7)} & = \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-baaef671-af9a-562b-9900-23310ee68578"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-baaef671-af9a-562b-9900-23310ee68578" :answer-field/id #uuid "baaef671-af9a-562b-9900-23310ee68578" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-48b80554-80e9-51c6-ba68-7718499fcfe4" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f3a55a62-0c0c-5220-b8c6-af6e6528b232" "answer-1159a985-d3e7-5ea5-9edc-8cb0dc94ade4" "answer-48b80554-80e9-51c6-ba68-7718499fcfe4" "answer-f76ea64e-21fc-595c-abaa-ccb166764691" "answer-5e0fa41d-14f0-57f7-a37b-2be2a23d1b25"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f3a55a62-0c0c-5220-b8c6-af6e6528b232" :answer/id #uuid "f3a55a62-0c0c-5220-b8c6-af6e6528b232" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-7\\sqrt{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1159a985-d3e7-5ea5-9edc-8cb0dc94ade4" :answer/id #uuid "1159a985-d3e7-5ea5-9edc-8cb0dc94ade4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{11}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-48b80554-80e9-51c6-ba68-7718499fcfe4" :answer/id #uuid "48b80554-80e9-51c6-ba68-7718499fcfe4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{7}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f76ea64e-21fc-595c-abaa-ccb166764691" :answer/id #uuid "f76ea64e-21fc-595c-abaa-ccb166764691" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "11\\sqrt{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5e0fa41d-14f0-57f7-a37b-2be2a23d1b25" :answer/id #uuid "5e0fa41d-14f0-57f7-a37b-2be2a23d1b25" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{7}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-2ea1efeb-69b1-5612-9cf2-036911016adf" :activity/id #uuid "2ea1efeb-69b1-5612-9cf2-036911016adf" :activity/title "Evaluating Numerical Expressions Containing Radicals" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186049299 :activity/course 17592186046648 :activity/steps ["step-30121170-ceeb-5f69-b1ac-9d4610650acd" "step-d23d9830-b6a5-59a5-aa4e-e7f66c2ee968" "step-11770b4d-89ac-52fa-8300-d5860f41b978" "step-7aee5440-5893-5f62-9295-e12330462be6" "step-97bed9e8-3ea8-54b5-9f95-1723f52f9663"] :activity/first-step "step-30121170-ceeb-5f69-b1ac-9d4610650acd" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-30121170-ceeb-5f69-b1ac-9d4610650acd" :step/id #uuid "30121170-ceeb-5f69-b1ac-9d4610650acd" :step/content "question-9d8a210d-995e-535a-9fa6-41cb0df374c0" :step/next "step-d23d9830-b6a5-59a5-aa4e-e7f66c2ee968" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-d23d9830-b6a5-59a5-aa4e-e7f66c2ee968" :step/id #uuid "d23d9830-b6a5-59a5-aa4e-e7f66c2ee968" :step/content "question-a7033116-7f31-505d-9b30-3f70773f08f3" :step/next "step-11770b4d-89ac-52fa-8300-d5860f41b978" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-11770b4d-89ac-52fa-8300-d5860f41b978" :step/id #uuid "11770b4d-89ac-52fa-8300-d5860f41b978" :step/content "question-5affd848-4f5d-5878-823f-f10731947773" :step/next "step-7aee5440-5893-5f62-9295-e12330462be6" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-7aee5440-5893-5f62-9295-e12330462be6" :step/id #uuid "7aee5440-5893-5f62-9295-e12330462be6" :step/content "question-50acaf8f-e65d-537d-be92-5374b0715e0a" :step/next "step-97bed9e8-3ea8-54b5-9f95-1723f52f9663" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-97bed9e8-3ea8-54b5-9f95-1723f52f9663" :step/id #uuid "97bed9e8-3ea8-54b5-9f95-1723f52f9663" :step/content "question-22c774c3-0c22-5336-81d9-8b5ada9d82d8" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186103846 :answer-field/choices 17592186103847] [:db/retract 17592186103846 :answer-field/choices 17592186103848] [:db/retract 17592186103846 :answer-field/choices 17592186103849] [:db/retract 17592186103846 :answer-field/choices 17592186103850] [:db/retract 17592186103846 :answer-field/choices 17592186103851] [:db/retract 17592186102282 :answer-field/choices 17592186102283] [:db/retract 17592186102282 :answer-field/choices 17592186102284] [:db/retract 17592186102282 :answer-field/choices 17592186102285] [:db/retract 17592186102282 :answer-field/choices 17592186102286] [:db/retract 17592186102282 :answer-field/choices 17592186102287] {:db/id 17592186105898 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-fae33bcb-2652-544e-838d-9fea7f4029a9"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186106283 :question/problem "Consider the straight line that passes through $P(-2,5,-3)$ and is parallel to $v=⟨1,-1,2⟩.$ Find all the possible points $A$ on that line such that $AP=\\sqrt{24}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the vector equation of the line, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =p+tv \\\\ r & =⟨-2,5,-3⟩+t⟨1,-1,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nSince $A$ lies on the line, we know that there must be a real number $t$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OA} & =⟨-2,5,-3⟩+t⟨1,-1,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PA} & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =(⟨-2,5,-3⟩+t⟨1,-1,2⟩)-⟨-2,5,-3⟩ \\\\  & =t\\,⟨1,-1,2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨t,\\,-t,\\,2t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $PA=\\sqrt{24},$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AP & =\\sqrt{24} \\\\ |\\overset{\\to }{PA}| & =\\sqrt{24} \\\\ |⟨t,\\,-t,\\,2t⟩| & =\\sqrt{24} \\\\ \\sqrt{{t}^{2}+(-t)^{2}+(2t)^{2}} & =\\sqrt{24} \\\\ \\sqrt{6{t}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{24} \\\\ \\pm t\\sqrt{6} & =2\\sqrt{6} \\\\ t & =\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the values $t=\\pm 2$ into the equation for the line, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\overset{\\to }{O{A}_{1}}=⟨-2,5,-3⟩+2⟨1,-1,2⟩=⟨0,3,1⟩, \\\\  & \\overset{\\to }{O{A}_{2}}=⟨-2,5,-3⟩-2⟨1,-1,2⟩=⟨-4,7,-7⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the points are ${A}_{1}(0,3,1)$ and ${A}_{2}(-4,7,-7).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186103846 :answer-field/correct "answer-8d8daf29-4004-5012-a711-688c5f2c743f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-2ca1ce6f-1cfa-5862-abce-de665c4b16ac" "answer-9028b822-283b-56d0-899a-4c8406bf3508" "answer-8d8daf29-4004-5012-a711-688c5f2c743f" "answer-aeea0b74-9973-5027-885f-71438bcfb05f" "answer-92e3c2fb-3736-54ce-91a9-4ddf0df4c5ee"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2ca1ce6f-1cfa-5862-abce-de665c4b16ac" :answer/id #uuid "2ca1ce6f-1cfa-5862-abce-de665c4b16ac" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${A}_{1}(-3,9,-4)$ and ${A}_{2}(-5,11,-8)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9028b822-283b-56d0-899a-4c8406bf3508" :answer/id #uuid "9028b822-283b-56d0-899a-4c8406bf3508" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${A}_{1}(-3,9,-4)$ and ${A}_{2}(5,-11,8)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8d8daf29-4004-5012-a711-688c5f2c743f" :answer/id #uuid "8d8daf29-4004-5012-a711-688c5f2c743f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${A}_{1}(0,3,1)$ and ${A}_{2}(-4,7,-7)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-aeea0b74-9973-5027-885f-71438bcfb05f" :answer/id #uuid "aeea0b74-9973-5027-885f-71438bcfb05f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${A}_{1}(0,3,1)$ and ${A}_{2}(4,-7,7)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-92e3c2fb-3736-54ce-91a9-4ddf0df4c5ee" :answer/id #uuid "92e3c2fb-3736-54ce-91a9-4ddf0df4c5ee" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${A}_{1}(4,-4,8)$ and ${A}_{2}(-4,4,-8)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-fae33bcb-2652-544e-838d-9fea7f4029a9" :question/id #uuid "fae33bcb-2652-544e-838d-9fea7f4029a9" :question/math-academy-id "q-69799" :question/problem "Consider the straight line that passes through $P(-4,2,-3)$ and is parallel to $v=⟨1,\\,0,\\,2⟩.$ Find all the possible points $X$ on that line such that $XP=\\sqrt{20}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the vector equation of the line, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =p+tv \\\\ r & =⟨-4,\\,2,\\,-3⟩+t⟨1,\\,0,\\,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nSince $X$ lies on the line, we know that there must be a real number $t$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OX} & =⟨-4,\\,2,\\,-3⟩+t⟨1,\\,0,\\,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PX} & =\\overset{\\to }{OX}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =(⟨-4,\\,2,\\,-3⟩+t⟨1,\\,0,\\,2⟩)-⟨-4,\\,2,\\,-3⟩ \\\\  & =t⟨1,\\,0,\\,2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨t,\\,0,\\,2t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $PX=\\sqrt{20},$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}XP & =\\sqrt{20} \\\\ |\\overset{\\to }{PX}| & =\\sqrt{20} \\\\ |⟨t,\\,0,\\,2t⟩| & =\\sqrt{20} \\\\ \\sqrt{{t}^{2}+{0}^{2}+(2t)^{2}} & =\\sqrt{20} \\\\ \\sqrt{5{t}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{20} \\\\ \\pm \\sqrt{5}t & =2\\sqrt{5} \\\\ t & =\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the values $t=\\pm 2$ into the equation for the line, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\overset{\\to }{O{X}_{1}}=⟨-4,\\,2,\\,-3⟩+2⟨1,\\,0,\\,2⟩=⟨-2,2,1⟩, \\\\  & \\overset{\\to }{O{X}_{2}}=⟨-4,\\,2,\\,-3⟩-2⟨1,\\,0,\\,2⟩=⟨-6,2,-7⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the points are ${X}_{1}(-2,2,1)$ and ${X}_{2}(-6,2,-7).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-5db99a8f-cee5-52ce-ae30-3cf87758843e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-5db99a8f-cee5-52ce-ae30-3cf87758843e" :answer-field/id #uuid "5db99a8f-cee5-52ce-ae30-3cf87758843e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-53ee61a5-d475-57b8-a0b9-a60723219d05" :answer-field/choices ["answer-bf682358-c577-5e53-95cb-2e1a7d3008bd" "answer-53ee61a5-d475-57b8-a0b9-a60723219d05" "answer-d6f87627-6a47-5a19-b342-cf1d6b482b3d" "answer-44125a3b-2563-5069-8bbc-ae69a62e4b8c" "answer-1bce919e-a0d8-59f0-8778-d5b453d280a8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bf682358-c577-5e53-95cb-2e1a7d3008bd" :answer/id #uuid "bf682358-c577-5e53-95cb-2e1a7d3008bd" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${X}_{1}(-2,0,1)$ and ${X}_{2}(1,2,-8)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-53ee61a5-d475-57b8-a0b9-a60723219d05" :answer/id #uuid "53ee61a5-d475-57b8-a0b9-a60723219d05" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${X}_{1}(-2,2,1)$ and ${X}_{2}(-6,2,-7)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d6f87627-6a47-5a19-b342-cf1d6b482b3d" :answer/id #uuid "d6f87627-6a47-5a19-b342-cf1d6b482b3d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${X}_{1}(-2,4,-1)$ and ${X}_{2}(2,4,-6)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-44125a3b-2563-5069-8bbc-ae69a62e4b8c" :answer/id #uuid "44125a3b-2563-5069-8bbc-ae69a62e4b8c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${X}_{1}(1,2,-1)$ and ${X}_{2}(-6,3,-9)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1bce919e-a0d8-59f0-8778-d5b453d280a8" :answer/id #uuid "1bce919e-a0d8-59f0-8778-d5b453d280a8" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${X}_{1}(2,-2,1)$ and ${X}_{2}(-4,-2,-5)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186105897 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-ba6720d7-134d-5884-b22f-7f4012ec3a77"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-ba6720d7-134d-5884-b22f-7f4012ec3a77" :question/id #uuid "ba6720d7-134d-5884-b22f-7f4012ec3a77" :question/math-academy-id "q-126964" :question/problem "Find a vector equation of the line that passes through the points $H(1,-2,3)$ and $S(4,-2,1).$" :question/worked-solution "The vector equation of the straight line passing through $H$ and parallel to $v$ is given by\n\n$$\nr=h+tv,\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ and $h$ is the position vector of $H.$ \n\nWe can find a direction vector by computing the displacement vector between the given points $H(1,-2,3)$ and $S(4,-2,1),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\overset{\\to }{HS} \\\\  & =⟨4,-2,1⟩-⟨1,-2,3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,0,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector equation of the line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-97d4e9ea-3206-52e6-90bd-f7265f05e6b9"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-97d4e9ea-3206-52e6-90bd-f7265f05e6b9" :answer-field/id #uuid "97d4e9ea-3206-52e6-90bd-f7265f05e6b9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-bafb0fb7-23f1-5dd9-8af3-0d84685adcd4" :answer-field/choices ["answer-3e3dc15f-f6a0-5469-bd09-5e5682833602" "answer-076aefd7-f3ef-5e65-9a6e-fcef6787013e" "answer-bafb0fb7-23f1-5dd9-8af3-0d84685adcd4" "answer-45c99e89-541a-51a5-acd4-9fe79f6c950f" "answer-31f26793-455b-5519-8bca-61ab48aea421"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3e3dc15f-f6a0-5469-bd09-5e5682833602" :answer/id #uuid "3e3dc15f-f6a0-5469-bd09-5e5682833602" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "r=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-076aefd7-f3ef-5e65-9a6e-fcef6787013e" :answer/id #uuid "076aefd7-f3ef-5e65-9a6e-fcef6787013e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "r=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bafb0fb7-23f1-5dd9-8af3-0d84685adcd4" :answer/id #uuid "bafb0fb7-23f1-5dd9-8af3-0d84685adcd4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "r=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-45c99e89-541a-51a5-acd4-9fe79f6c950f" :answer/id #uuid "45c99e89-541a-51a5-acd4-9fe79f6c950f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "r=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-31f26793-455b-5519-8bca-61ab48aea421" :answer/id #uuid "31f26793-455b-5519-8bca-61ab48aea421" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "r=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186106066 :question/problem "The equation below gives a parametrization of the line segment $\\overset{―}{PQ},$ where $P(1,0,3)$ and $Q(1,2,0).$\n\n$$\nr(t)=i+a\\,j+b\\,k,\\,t\\in [c,1]\n$$\n\nWhat is $a+b+c?$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find a direction vector that is parallel to the given line. We can use the vector $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\overset{\\to }{PQ} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{OQ}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =(i+2\\,j)-(i+3\\,k) \\\\  & =2\\,j-3\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\nr=p+tv\\,⟹\\,r=(i+3\\,k)+t(2\\,j-3\\,k)\n$$\n\nand the parametrization of the line segment $\\overset{―}{PQ}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =(i+3\\,k)+t(2\\,j-3\\,k) \\\\  & =i+2t\\,j+(3-3t)\\,k,\\,t\\in [0,1].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=2t+(3-3t)+0=3-t.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186102282 :answer-field/correct "answer-88a898e9-97f9-5c0d-a2a9-e984a3785f23" :answer-field/choices ["answer-12c0bd9a-8112-53b2-b018-8f7e3b0e9cac" "answer-8f4c0811-7dac-5fed-9809-20000ee340e3" "answer-88a898e9-97f9-5c0d-a2a9-e984a3785f23" "answer-6ddce68d-485d-5026-a8d0-15c7aadcd2ce" "answer-9c7163c1-8542-5ca0-8417-6dfd8942bd73"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-12c0bd9a-8112-53b2-b018-8f7e3b0e9cac" :answer/id #uuid "12c0bd9a-8112-53b2-b018-8f7e3b0e9cac" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1+2t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8f4c0811-7dac-5fed-9809-20000ee340e3" :answer/id #uuid "8f4c0811-7dac-5fed-9809-20000ee340e3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3-5{t}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-88a898e9-97f9-5c0d-a2a9-e984a3785f23" :answer/id #uuid "88a898e9-97f9-5c0d-a2a9-e984a3785f23" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3-t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6ddce68d-485d-5026-a8d0-15c7aadcd2ce" :answer/id #uuid "6ddce68d-485d-5026-a8d0-15c7aadcd2ce" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4-2t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9c7163c1-8542-5ca0-8417-6dfd8942bd73" :answer/id #uuid "9c7163c1-8542-5ca0-8417-6dfd8942bd73" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{t}^{2}-2t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-cdd38161-b7a8-5f07-98fd-ab57d6d2c3c9" :activity/id #uuid "cdd38161-b7a8-5f07-98fd-ab57d6d2c3c9" :activity/title "The Vector Equation of a Line" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186049485 :activity/course 17592186046659 :activity/steps ["step-bb116c13-04f6-5718-b5fc-2b6fa8103e9e" "step-3b25e9df-26b3-594e-b7e5-ee4df36a2c1f" "step-1ec7ec1f-8794-50d0-a0c9-c532caebad39" "step-4cb265da-0c2e-574b-9b97-7e10561660e5" "step-fa1f6cbe-7cdd-5cc9-a956-7908cb978446"] :activity/first-step "step-bb116c13-04f6-5718-b5fc-2b6fa8103e9e" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-bb116c13-04f6-5718-b5fc-2b6fa8103e9e" :step/id #uuid "bb116c13-04f6-5718-b5fc-2b6fa8103e9e" :step/content 17592186106283 :step/next "step-3b25e9df-26b3-594e-b7e5-ee4df36a2c1f" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-3b25e9df-26b3-594e-b7e5-ee4df36a2c1f" :step/id #uuid "3b25e9df-26b3-594e-b7e5-ee4df36a2c1f" :step/content "question-fae33bcb-2652-544e-838d-9fea7f4029a9" :step/next "step-1ec7ec1f-8794-50d0-a0c9-c532caebad39" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-1ec7ec1f-8794-50d0-a0c9-c532caebad39" :step/id #uuid "1ec7ec1f-8794-50d0-a0c9-c532caebad39" :step/content "question-ba6720d7-134d-5884-b22f-7f4012ec3a77" :step/next "step-4cb265da-0c2e-574b-9b97-7e10561660e5" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-4cb265da-0c2e-574b-9b97-7e10561660e5" :step/id #uuid "4cb265da-0c2e-574b-9b97-7e10561660e5" :step/content 17592186106066 :step/next "step-fa1f6cbe-7cdd-5cc9-a956-7908cb978446" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-fa1f6cbe-7cdd-5cc9-a956-7908cb978446" :step/id #uuid "fa1f6cbe-7cdd-5cc9-a956-7908cb978446" :step/content 17592186281392 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186099561 :answer-field/choices 17592186099562] [:db/retract 17592186099561 :answer-field/choices 17592186099563] [:db/retract 17592186099561 :answer-field/choices 17592186099564] [:db/retract 17592186099561 :answer-field/choices 17592186099565] [:db/retract 17592186099561 :answer-field/choices 17592186099566] {:db/id 17592186100805 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-6f10ffe0-480f-5e92-a4dc-edbf0f536c4b" "question-eda5d85b-de52-5d2f-abad-d370d89d2617"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186100715 :question/problem "What is the missing number?\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} &  &  &  &  &  & 1 \\\\  &  &  &  &  & 1 &  & 1 \\\\  &  &  &  & 1 &  & 2 &  & 1 \\\\  &  &  & 1 &  & 3 &  & 3 &  & 1 \\\\  &  & 1 &  & ◻ &  & 6 &  & 4 &  & 1 \\\\  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "In Pascal's triangle, the first and last number in each row is $1.$ The other numbers are always greater than $1$ and can be found by adding the two closest numbers located in the previous row.\n\nIn our case, the numbers closest to the unknown number and located in the previous row are $1$ and $3.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} &  &  &  &  &  & 1 \\\\  &  &  &  &  & 1 &  & 1 \\\\  &  &  &  & 1 &  & 2 &  & 1 \\\\  &  &  & 1 &  & 3 &  & 3 &  & 1 \\\\  &  & 1 &  & ◻ &  & 6 &  & 4 &  & 1 \\\\  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the unknown number is the sum of $1$ and $3\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n{\\large\\square} = 1 + 3 = 4\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186100806 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-cb084350-7fbe-5022-a4f9-36da32efbecb"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186100716 :question/problem "Expand the binomial $(2z + 1)^4.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's compare our expression with the binomial\n\n$$\n(a+b)^n.\n$$\n\nWe have that $n=4,$ $a  = 2z,$ and $b= 1.$ So, the exponents of the binomial expansion must follow the pattern\n\n$$\na^4b^0,\\quad  a^{3}b^1,\\quad  a^{2}b^2,\\quad  a^1b^3,\\quad  a^0b^4.\n$$\n\nThe $4$th row in Pascal's Triangle is $(1,4,6,4,1).$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t(2z + 1)^4   &=\\mathbf{1}a^4 + \\mathbf{4}a^3b + \\mathbf{6}a^2b^2 + \\mathbf{4}ab^3 + \\mathbf{1}b^4\\[5pt]\n&=\\mathbf{1}(2z)^4(1)^0 + \\mathbf{4}(2z)^3(1)^1 + \\mathbf{6}(2z)^2(1)^2 + \\mathbf{4}(2z)^1(1)^3 + \\mathbf{1}(2z)^0(1)^4\\[5pt]\n\n&=(2z)^4 + 4(2z)^3 + 6(2z)^2 + 4(2z) +1\\[5pt]\n\n&=16z^4 + 32z^3 + 24z^2 + 8z + 1\n\n                       \n\n\t}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186100807 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-ab2cb57c-ba4d-5126-ab49-971b151088ab" "question-3050d934-f604-5f69-a586-6c218326013a" "question-ac49f7f6-9f77-5cda-ac3b-cdee9bcbb73f"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186100719 :question/problem "Expand the binomial $(3x-y)^4.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's compare our expression with the binomial\n\n$$\n(a+b)^n.\n$$\n\nHere we have that $n=4,$ $a  = 3x,$ and $b= -y.$ So, the exponents of the binomial expansion must follow the pattern\n\n$$\na^4b^0,\\quad  a^{3}b^1,\\quad  a^{2}b^2,\\quad  a^1b^3,\\quad  a^0b^4.\n$$\n\nThe $4$th row in Pascal's Triangle is $(1,4,6,4,1).$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t(3x - y)^4   \n&=\\mathbf{1}a^4 + \\mathbf{4}a^3b + \\mathbf{6}a^2b^2 + \\mathbf{4}ab^3 + \\mathbf{1}b^4\\[5pt]\n&=\n\t\\mathbf{1}(3x)^4(-y)^0 + \\mathbf{4}(3x)^3(-y)^1 + \\mathbf{6}(3x)^2(-y)^2 + \\mathbf{4}(3x)^1(-y)^3 + \\mathbf{1}(3x)^0(-y)^4\\[5pt]\n                          &=\n\t\\mathbf{1}\\left(81x^4\\right)(1) + \\mathbf{4}\\left(27x^3\\right)(-y) + \\mathbf{6}\\left(9x^2\\right)(y^2) + \\mathbf{4}(3x)(-y^3) + \\mathbf{1}(1)(y^4) \\[5pt]\n                         &=  81x^4 - 108x^3y + 54x^2y^2 - 12xy^3 + y^4.\n\t}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186100808 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-bb756db8-08d4-574e-9721-71fce97c078b" "question-7cdcec28-9eea-5d7b-863d-1e35767ec2c5" "question-583748e6-2c8b-5634-bc36-5084c91f8d81" "question-1561558e-21f5-576d-9b7c-91bcf7619901"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186100720 :question/problem "Find the coefficient of $x^4y$ in the expansion of  ${(2x^2+y)}^3 .$" :question/worked-solution "Since the exponent of the binomial expression is $n=3,$ we see that the exponents of the binomial expansion must follow the pattern\n\n$$\n\\left(2x^2\\right)^3y^0, \\qquad {\\color{blue}{\\left(2x^2\\right)^2 y^1}},\\qquad \\left(2x^2\\right)^1 y^2,\\qquad \\left(2x^2\\right)^0 y^3.\n$$\n\nOf these, the term that returns $x^4y$ is the second. So, we are looking for the second term in the binomial expansion.\n\nThe $3$rd row in Pascal's Triangle is $(1,{\\color{red}3},3,1).$ Therefore, the second term in the binomial expansion is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{(2{x}^{2})}^{2}{(y)}^{1} & =3(4{x}^{4})(y) \\\\  & =12{x}^{4}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the coefficient of $x^4y$ in the expansion of ${(2x^2+y)}^3$ is $12.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-6f10ffe0-480f-5e92-a4dc-edbf0f536c4b" :question/id #uuid "6f10ffe0-480f-5e92-a4dc-edbf0f536c4b" :question/math-academy-id "q-110576" :question/problem "Express the missing number as the sum of two numbers from Pascal's triangle.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} &  &  &  &  &  &  &  & 1 \\\\  &  &  &  &  &  &  & 1 &  & 1 \\\\  &  &  &  &  &  & 1 &  & 2 &  & 1 \\\\  &  &  &  &  & 1 &  & 3 &  & 3 &  & 1 \\\\  &  &  &  & 1 &  & 4 &  & 6 &  & 4 &  & 1 \\\\  &  &  & 1 &  & 5 &  & 10 &  & 10 &  & 5 &  & 1 \\\\  &  & 1 &  & 6 &  & 15 &  & 20 &  & 15 &  & 6 &  & 1 \\\\  & 1 &  & 7 &  & ◻ &  & 35 &  & 35 &  & 21 &  & 7 &  & 1 \\\\ ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "In Pascal's triangle, the first and last number in each row is $1.$ The other numbers are always greater than $1$ and can be found by adding the two closest numbers located in the previous row.\n\nIn our case, the numbers closest to the unknown number and located in the previous row are $6$ and $15.$\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} &  &  &  &  &  &  &  & 1 \\\\  &  &  &  &  &  &  & 1 &  & 1 \\\\  &  &  &  &  &  & 1 &  & 2 &  & 1 \\\\  &  &  &  &  & 1 &  & 3 &  & 3 &  & 1 \\\\  &  &  &  & 1 &  & 4 &  & 6 &  & 4 &  & 1 \\\\  &  &  & 1 &  & 5 &  & 10 &  & 10 &  & 5 &  & 1 \\\\  &  & 1 &  & 6 &  & 15 &  & 20 &  & 15 &  & 6 &  & 1 \\\\  & 1 &  & 7 &  & ◻ &  & 35 &  & 35 &  & 21 &  & 7 &  & 1 \\\\ ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nTherefore, the unknown number is the sum of $6$ and $15.$ \n\n$$\n◻=6+15\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-a66ad25d-aec4-563a-8849-bb0750298a42"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-a66ad25d-aec4-563a-8849-bb0750298a42" :answer-field/id #uuid "a66ad25d-aec4-563a-8849-bb0750298a42" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-5409ef18-5e70-5adb-addc-1ae48ed88a68" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e1d23774-6298-5410-9fe3-6ab519fa526b" "answer-07a3b48b-b9d3-5daf-b3b0-f5f4f566089f" "answer-e4561f9e-0bc2-5584-8ae3-7e0ee952d2ae" "answer-5409ef18-5e70-5adb-addc-1ae48ed88a68" "answer-53f3072f-edd2-5544-9c6b-72edc6e7c1f8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e1d23774-6298-5410-9fe3-6ab519fa526b" :answer/id #uuid "e1d23774-6298-5410-9fe3-6ab519fa526b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1+6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-07a3b48b-b9d3-5daf-b3b0-f5f4f566089f" :answer/id #uuid "07a3b48b-b9d3-5daf-b3b0-f5f4f566089f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "15+20" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e4561f9e-0bc2-5584-8ae3-7e0ee952d2ae" :answer/id #uuid "e4561f9e-0bc2-5584-8ae3-7e0ee952d2ae" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5+10" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5409ef18-5e70-5adb-addc-1ae48ed88a68" :answer/id #uuid "5409ef18-5e70-5adb-addc-1ae48ed88a68" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6+15" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-53f3072f-edd2-5544-9c6b-72edc6e7c1f8" :answer/id #uuid "53f3072f-edd2-5544-9c6b-72edc6e7c1f8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7+35" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-eda5d85b-de52-5d2f-abad-d370d89d2617" :question/id #uuid "eda5d85b-de52-5d2f-abad-d370d89d2617" :question/math-academy-id "q-110580" :question/problem "What is the missing number?\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} &  &  &  &  &  &  &  & 1 \\\\  &  &  &  &  &  &  & 1 &  & 1 \\\\  &  &  &  &  &  & 1 &  & 2 &  & 1 \\\\  &  &  &  &  & 1 &  & 3 &  & 3 &  & 1 \\\\  &  &  &  & 1 &  & 4 &  & 6 &  & 4 &  & 1 \\\\  &  &  & 1 &  & 5 &  & 10 &  & 10 &  & 5 &  & 1 \\\\  &  & 1 &  & 6 &  & 15 &  & 20 &  & 15 &  & 6 &  & 1 \\\\  & 1 &  & 7 &  & 21 &  & 35 &  & ◻ &  & 21 &  & 7 &  & 1 \\\\ ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "In Pascal's triangle, the first and last number in each row is $1.$ The other numbers are always greater than $1$ and can be found by adding the two closest numbers located in the previous row.\n\nIn our case, the numbers closest to the unknown number and located in the previous row are $20$ and $15.$\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} &  &  &  &  &  &  &  & 1 \\\\  &  &  &  &  &  &  & 1 &  & 1 \\\\  &  &  &  &  &  & 1 &  & 2 &  & 1 \\\\  &  &  &  &  & 1 &  & 3 &  & 3 &  & 1 \\\\  &  &  &  & 1 &  & 4 &  & 6 &  & 4 &  & 1 \\\\  &  &  & 1 &  & 5 &  & 10 &  & 10 &  & 5 &  & 1 \\\\  &  & 1 &  & 6 &  & 15 &  & 20 &  & 15 &  & 6 &  & 1 \\\\  & 1 &  & 7 &  & 21 &  & 35 &  & ◻ &  & 21 &  & 7 &  & 1 \\\\ ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nTherefore, the unknown number is the sum of $20$ and $15.$ \n\n$$\n◻=20+15=35\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-56439851-b1a9-5024-aa32-d6bde6a4795a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-56439851-b1a9-5024-aa32-d6bde6a4795a" :answer-field/id #uuid "56439851-b1a9-5024-aa32-d6bde6a4795a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-1c3312b2-946c-52e6-9c9f-af514b378a9f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d402dc28-4222-5370-b573-d4108f999d2e" "answer-29ec2521-1737-586e-9cd8-2b63fd0d9df2" "answer-3faba281-fb11-5e0f-b9cd-5d4ab53cb80b" "answer-1c3312b2-946c-52e6-9c9f-af514b378a9f" "answer-bd450e1b-1c56-5803-8bf0-3d3fa6025dfb"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d402dc28-4222-5370-b573-d4108f999d2e" :answer/id #uuid "d402dc28-4222-5370-b573-d4108f999d2e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "15" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-29ec2521-1737-586e-9cd8-2b63fd0d9df2" :answer/id #uuid "29ec2521-1737-586e-9cd8-2b63fd0d9df2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "20" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3faba281-fb11-5e0f-b9cd-5d4ab53cb80b" :answer/id #uuid "3faba281-fb11-5e0f-b9cd-5d4ab53cb80b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "21" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1c3312b2-946c-52e6-9c9f-af514b378a9f" :answer/id #uuid "1c3312b2-946c-52e6-9c9f-af514b378a9f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "35" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bd450e1b-1c56-5803-8bf0-3d3fa6025dfb" :answer/id #uuid "bd450e1b-1c56-5803-8bf0-3d3fa6025dfb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "56" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-cb084350-7fbe-5022-a4f9-36da32efbecb" :question/id #uuid "cb084350-7fbe-5022-a4f9-36da32efbecb" :question/math-academy-id "q-110589" :question/problem "Find, in descending powers of $t,$ the first three terms of the binomial expansion of $(t+2)^{4}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's compare our expression with the binomial\n\n$$\n(a+b)^{n}.\n$$\n\nHere we have that $n=4,$ $a=t,$ and $b=2.$ So, the exponents of the binomial expansion must follow the pattern\n\n$$\n{a}^{4}{b}^{0},\\,{a}^{3}{b}^{1},\\,{a}^{2}{b}^{2},\\,{a}^{1}{b}^{3},\\,{a}^{0}{b}^{4}.\n$$\n\nThe $4$th row in Pascal's Triangle is $(1,4,6,4,1).$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(t+2)^{4} & =1{a}^{4}{b}^{0}+4{a}^{3}b+6{a}^{2}{b}^{2}+4a{b}^{3}+1{b}^{4} \\\\  & =1(t)^{4}(2)^{0}+4(t)^{3}(2)^{1}+6(t)^{2}(2)^{2}+4(t)^{1}(2)^{3}+1(t)^{0}(2)^{4} \\\\  & =1({t}^{4})(1)+4({t}^{3})(2)+6({t}^{2})({2}^{2})+4(t)({2}^{3})+1({t}^{0})({2}^{4}) \\\\  & ={t}^{4}+8{t}^{3}+24{t}^{2}+32t+16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, in descending powers of $t,$ the first three terms of the binomial expansion are\n\n$$\n{t}^{4}+8{t}^{3}+24{t}^{2}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-41287685-a6c0-5218-984a-5454e51a8b4b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-41287685-a6c0-5218-984a-5454e51a8b4b" :answer-field/id #uuid "41287685-a6c0-5218-984a-5454e51a8b4b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-bf864856-3bd9-553f-91fa-c9242f57bf1e" :answer-field/choices ["answer-07a0b4fd-3612-587d-b648-d556fa08fa5e" "answer-d754a224-720e-52be-a22a-203492daf179" "answer-03bf8b50-3f39-5a0e-a1f9-19cc26b4e8e5" "answer-32d9dbb4-f28d-504e-a247-585aad76ea3e" "answer-bf864856-3bd9-553f-91fa-c9242f57bf1e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-07a0b4fd-3612-587d-b648-d556fa08fa5e" :answer/id #uuid "07a0b4fd-3612-587d-b648-d556fa08fa5e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{t}^{4}+16{t}^{3}+24{t}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d754a224-720e-52be-a22a-203492daf179" :answer/id #uuid "d754a224-720e-52be-a22a-203492daf179" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{t}^{4}+32{t}^{3}+96{t}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-03bf8b50-3f39-5a0e-a1f9-19cc26b4e8e5" :answer/id #uuid "03bf8b50-3f39-5a0e-a1f9-19cc26b4e8e5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{t}^{4}+4{t}^{3}+6{t}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-32d9dbb4-f28d-504e-a247-585aad76ea3e" :answer/id #uuid "32d9dbb4-f28d-504e-a247-585aad76ea3e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{t}^{4}+8{t}^{3}+12{t}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bf864856-3bd9-553f-91fa-c9242f57bf1e" :answer/id #uuid "bf864856-3bd9-553f-91fa-c9242f57bf1e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{t}^{4}+8{t}^{3}+24{t}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186101400 :question/problem "Find, in ascending powers of $n,$ the first three terms of the binomial expansion of $(2-5n)^{4}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's compare our expression with the binomial\n\n$$\n(a+b)^{n}.\n$$\n\nHere we have that $n=4,$ $a=2,$ and $b=-5n.$ So, the exponents of the binomial expansion must follow the pattern\n\n$$\n{a}^{4}{b}^{0},\\,{a}^{3}{b}^{1},\\,{a}^{2}{b}^{2},\\,{a}^{1}{b}^{3},\\,{a}^{0}{b}^{4}.\n$$\n\nThe $4$th row in Pascal's Triangle is $(1,4,6,4,1).$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2-5n)^{4} & =1{a}^{4}+4{a}^{3}b+6{a}^{2}{b}^{2}+4a{b}^{3}+1{b}^{4} \\\\  & =1(2)^{4}(-5n)^{0}+4(2)^{3}(-5n)^{1}+6(2)^{2}(-5n)^{2}+4(2)(-5n)^{3}+1(2)^{0}(-5n)^{4} \\\\  & =1(16)(1)+4(8)(-5n)+6(4)(25{n}^{2})+4(2)(-125{n}^{3})+1(1)(625{n}^{4}) \\\\  & =16-160n+600{n}^{2}-1\\,000{n}^{3}+625{n}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, in ascending powers of $n$, the first three terms are\n\n$$\n16-160n+600{n}^{2}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186099561 :answer-field/correct "answer-31c931e7-744c-5a82-b705-86dc1a635969" :answer-field/choices ["answer-2283d911-bad8-5d7c-a1c0-284b5c6924b7" "answer-65a8aad5-c264-545f-a300-db72f7739c27" "answer-31c931e7-744c-5a82-b705-86dc1a635969" "answer-55179f7d-8d07-574b-80ee-6436c8610ea7" "answer-c441fca8-b3a3-51b0-9e25-d8495cda4c4f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2283d911-bad8-5d7c-a1c0-284b5c6924b7" :answer/id #uuid "2283d911-bad8-5d7c-a1c0-284b5c6924b7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "16-160n+300{n}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-65a8aad5-c264-545f-a300-db72f7739c27" :answer/id #uuid "65a8aad5-c264-545f-a300-db72f7739c27" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "16-160n+350{n}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-31c931e7-744c-5a82-b705-86dc1a635969" :answer/id #uuid "31c931e7-744c-5a82-b705-86dc1a635969" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "16-160n+600{n}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-55179f7d-8d07-574b-80ee-6436c8610ea7" :answer/id #uuid "55179f7d-8d07-574b-80ee-6436c8610ea7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "16-80n+300{n}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c441fca8-b3a3-51b0-9e25-d8495cda4c4f" :answer/id #uuid "c441fca8-b3a3-51b0-9e25-d8495cda4c4f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "16-80n+600{n}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-ab2cb57c-ba4d-5126-ab49-971b151088ab" :question/id #uuid "ab2cb57c-ba4d-5126-ab49-971b151088ab" :question/math-academy-id "q-3744" :question/problem "Given that \n\n$$\n{(r+m)}^{3}=A{r}^{3}+B{r}^{2}m+Cr{m}^{2}+{m}^{3}\n$$\n\nwhere $A,B$ and $C$ are constants, determine the value of $A+B+C.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's compare our expression with the binomial\n\n$$\n(a+b)^{n}.\n$$\n\nHere we have that $n=3,$ $a=r,$ and $b=m.$ So, the exponents of the binomial expansion must follow the pattern\n\n$$\n{a}^{3}{b}^{0},\\,{a}^{2}{b}^{1},\\,{a}^{1}{b}^{2},\\,{a}^{0}{b}^{3}.\n$$\n\nThe $3$rd row in Pascal's Triangle is $(1,3,3,1).$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r+m)^{3} & =1{a}^{3}+3{a}^{2}b+3a{b}^{2}+1{b}^{3} \\\\  & =1(r)^{3}(m)^{0}+3(r)^{2}(m)^{1}+3(r)^{1}(m)^{2}+1(r)^{0}(m)^{3} \\\\  & =1({r}^{3})(1)+3({r}^{2})(m)+3(r)({m}^{2})+1(1)({m}^{3}) \\\\  & ={r}^{3}+3{r}^{2}m+3r{m}^{2}+{m}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $A=1,B=3,$ and $C=3.$ Finally, then\n\n$$\nA+B+C=1+3+3=7.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-57b878df-ae21-5b04-a919-8ba7f8eb6a11"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-57b878df-ae21-5b04-a919-8ba7f8eb6a11" :answer-field/id #uuid "57b878df-ae21-5b04-a919-8ba7f8eb6a11" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-df61de6e-e6d3-5895-9840-de4d3b298884" :answer-field/choices ["answer-4bd18569-c173-59a1-bf20-338f78ed324f" "answer-42ee8705-56f2-5f77-8daf-070e169b799d" "answer-15d39e02-a5f2-5bb5-b092-37464400d565" "answer-df61de6e-e6d3-5895-9840-de4d3b298884" "answer-058905e9-4d11-5574-a85c-d2d747756cff"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4bd18569-c173-59a1-bf20-338f78ed324f" :answer/id #uuid "4bd18569-c173-59a1-bf20-338f78ed324f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "13" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-42ee8705-56f2-5f77-8daf-070e169b799d" :answer/id #uuid "42ee8705-56f2-5f77-8daf-070e169b799d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "19" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-15d39e02-a5f2-5bb5-b092-37464400d565" :answer/id #uuid "15d39e02-a5f2-5bb5-b092-37464400d565" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-df61de6e-e6d3-5895-9840-de4d3b298884" :answer/id #uuid "df61de6e-e6d3-5895-9840-de4d3b298884" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-058905e9-4d11-5574-a85c-d2d747756cff" :answer/id #uuid "058905e9-4d11-5574-a85c-d2d747756cff" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186101117 :question/problem "Given that \n\n$$\n{(y+{x}^{2})}^{3}={y}^{3}+A{y}^{2}{x}^{2}+By{x}^{4}+C{x}^{6}\n$$\n\nwhere $A,B$ and $C$ are constants, determine the value of $A+B+C.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's compare our expression with the binomial\n\n$$\n(a+b)^{n}.\n$$\n\nHere we have that $n=3,$ $a=y,$ and $b={x}^{2}.$ So, the exponents of the binomial expansion must follow the pattern\n\n$$\n{a}^{3}{b}^{0},\\,{a}^{2}{b}^{1},\\,{a}^{1}{b}^{2},\\,{a}^{0}{b}^{3}.\n$$\n\nThe $3$rd row in Pascal's Triangle is $(1,3,3,1).$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(y+{x}^{2})}^{3} & =1{a}^{3}+3{a}^{2}b+3a{b}^{2}+1{b}^{3} \\\\  & =1(y)^{3}{({x}^{2})}^{0}+3(y)^{2}{({x}^{2})}^{1}+3(y)^{1}{({x}^{2})}^{2}+1(y)^{0}{({x}^{2})}^{3} \\\\  & =1{y}^{3}(1)+3{y}^{2}{x}^{2}+3y{x}^{4}+1(1){x}^{6} \\\\  & ={y}^{3}+3{y}^{2}{x}^{2}+3y{x}^{4}+{x}^{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $A=3,B=3,$ and $C=1.$ Finally, then\n\n$$\nA+B+C=3+3+1=7.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186098037 :answer-field/correct 17592186098042 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-3050d934-f604-5f69-a586-6c218326013a" :question/id #uuid "3050d934-f604-5f69-a586-6c218326013a" :question/math-academy-id "q-12484" :question/problem "Find, in descending powers of $y$ and ascending powers of $z,$ the first three terms of the binomial expansion of $(y+z)^{4}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's compare our expression with the binomial\n\n$$\n(a+b)^{n}.\n$$\n\nHere we have that $n=4,$ $a=y,$ and $b=z.$ So, the exponents of the binomial expansion must follow the pattern\n\n$$\n{a}^{4}{b}^{0},\\,{a}^{3}{b}^{1},\\,{a}^{2}{b}^{2},\\,{a}^{1}{b}^{3},\\,{a}^{0}{b}^{4}.\n$$\n\nThe $4$th row in Pascal's Triangle is $(1,4,6,4,1).$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+z)^{4} & =1{a}^{4}+4{a}^{3}b+6{a}^{2}{b}^{2}+4a{b}^{3}+1{b}^{4} \\\\  & =1(y)^{4}(z)^{0}+4(y)^{3}(z)^{1}+6(y)^{2}(z)^{2}+4(y)^{1}(z)^{3}+1(y)^{0}(z)^{4} \\\\  & =1({y}^{4})(1)+4({y}^{3})(z)+6({y}^{2})({z}^{2})+4(y)({z}^{3})+1(1)({z}^{4}) \\\\  & ={y}^{4}+4{y}^{3}z+6{y}^{2}{z}^{2}+4y{z}^{3}+{z}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, in descending powers of $y$ and ascending powers of $z,$ the first three terms are\n\n$$\n{y}^{4}+4{y}^{3}z+6{y}^{2}{z}^{2}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-6ec8b379-7fc5-5e9a-a35d-30cbe0d09a9f"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-6ec8b379-7fc5-5e9a-a35d-30cbe0d09a9f" :answer-field/id #uuid "6ec8b379-7fc5-5e9a-a35d-30cbe0d09a9f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-1aaf67c6-84a3-529f-bd3a-02f8521fb4c0" :answer-field/choices ["answer-1096edf0-16af-5236-8173-4762378e1310" "answer-26b6a154-d7ce-5869-b7d3-8f755d415bee" "answer-634114ad-6c02-51f7-b56e-9745a18ff622" "answer-615ae422-8dba-5d0d-ab94-d66ebe11a101" "answer-1aaf67c6-84a3-529f-bd3a-02f8521fb4c0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1096edf0-16af-5236-8173-4762378e1310" :answer/id #uuid "1096edf0-16af-5236-8173-4762378e1310" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{4}+3{y}^{3}z+15{y}^{2}{z}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-26b6a154-d7ce-5869-b7d3-8f755d415bee" :answer/id #uuid "26b6a154-d7ce-5869-b7d3-8f755d415bee" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{4}+3{y}^{3}z+6{y}^{2}{z}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-634114ad-6c02-51f7-b56e-9745a18ff622" :answer/id #uuid "634114ad-6c02-51f7-b56e-9745a18ff622" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{4}+4{y}^{3}z+10{y}^{2}{z}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-615ae422-8dba-5d0d-ab94-d66ebe11a101" :answer/id #uuid "615ae422-8dba-5d0d-ab94-d66ebe11a101" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{4}+4{y}^{3}z+12{y}^{2}{z}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1aaf67c6-84a3-529f-bd3a-02f8521fb4c0" :answer/id #uuid "1aaf67c6-84a3-529f-bd3a-02f8521fb4c0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{4}+4{y}^{3}z+6{y}^{2}{z}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-ac49f7f6-9f77-5cda-ac3b-cdee9bcbb73f" :question/id #uuid "ac49f7f6-9f77-5cda-ac3b-cdee9bcbb73f" :question/math-academy-id "q-12892" :question/problem "Find, in ascending powers of $y$ and descending powers of $z,$ the first three terms of the binomial expansion of ${(\\frac{2y}{{z}^{2}}-{z}^{2})}^{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's compare our expression with the binomial\n\n$$\n(a+b)^{n}.\n$$\n\nHere we have that $n=3,$ $a=\\frac{2y}{{z}^{2}},$ and $b=-{z}^{2}.$ So, the exponents of the binomial expansion must follow the pattern\n\n$$\n{a}^{3}{b}^{0},\\,{a}^{2}{b}^{1},\\,{a}^{1}{b}^{2},\\,{a}^{0}{b}^{3}.\n$$\n\nThe $3$rd row in Pascal's Triangle is $(1,3,3,1).$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{2y}{{z}^{2}}-{z}^{2})}^{3} & =1{a}^{3}+3{a}^{2}b+3a{b}^{2}+1{b}^{3} \\\\  & =1{(\\frac{2y}{{z}^{2}})}^{3}{(-{z}^{2})}^{0}+3{(\\frac{2y}{{z}^{2}})}^{2}{(-{z}^{2})}^{1} \\\\  & \\,+3{(\\frac{2y}{{z}^{2}})}^{1}{(-{z}^{2})}^{2}+1{(\\frac{2y}{{z}^{2}})}^{0}{(-{z}^{2})}^{3} \\\\  & =1(\\frac{8{y}^{3}}{{z}^{6}})(1)+3(\\frac{4{y}^{2}}{{z}^{4}})(-{z}^{2}) \\\\  & \\,+3(\\frac{2y}{{z}^{2}})({z}^{4})+1(1)(-{z}^{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{8{y}^{3}}{{z}^{6}}-\\frac{12{y}^{2}{z}^{2}}{{z}^{4}}+\\frac{6y{z}^{4}}{{z}^{2}}-{z}^{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{8{y}^{3}}{{z}^{6}}-\\frac{12{y}^{2}}{{z}^{2}}+6y{z}^{2}-{z}^{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe rewrite the binomial expansion in ascending powers of $y$ and descending powers of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{2y}{{z}^{2}}-{z}^{2})}^{3}=-{z}^{6}+6y{z}^{2}-\\frac{12{y}^{2}}{{z}^{2}}+\\frac{8{y}^{3}}{{z}^{6}}\n$$\n\nFinally, in ascending powers of $y$ and descending powers of $z,$ the first three terms are\n\n$$\n-{z}^{6}+6y{z}^{2}-\\frac{12{y}^{2}}{{z}^{2}}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-6dfe3b1d-64a2-5aa6-947a-b91c6e82fc88"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-6dfe3b1d-64a2-5aa6-947a-b91c6e82fc88" :answer-field/id #uuid "6dfe3b1d-64a2-5aa6-947a-b91c6e82fc88" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-f70054c3-f419-516b-9432-262356961613" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f225180b-2833-5feb-9ee4-590d742c0ead" "answer-3ab7dbdf-cdeb-5d59-b993-8e3c334667e0" "answer-f70054c3-f419-516b-9432-262356961613" "answer-7f51ea30-b85b-5aaa-8db2-5c054cbf097c" "answer-9c65c1ea-1534-5429-9fa5-eb19d6c92c34"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f225180b-2833-5feb-9ee4-590d742c0ead" :answer/id #uuid "f225180b-2833-5feb-9ee4-590d742c0ead" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-{z}^{6}+12y{z}^{2}-\\frac{24{y}^{2}}{{z}^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3ab7dbdf-cdeb-5d59-b993-8e3c334667e0" :answer/id #uuid "3ab7dbdf-cdeb-5d59-b993-8e3c334667e0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-{z}^{6}+12y{z}^{2}-\\frac{6{y}^{2}}{{z}^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f70054c3-f419-516b-9432-262356961613" :answer/id #uuid "f70054c3-f419-516b-9432-262356961613" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-{z}^{6}+6y{z}^{2}-\\frac{12{y}^{2}}{{z}^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7f51ea30-b85b-5aaa-8db2-5c054cbf097c" :answer/id #uuid "7f51ea30-b85b-5aaa-8db2-5c054cbf097c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-{z}^{6}+6y{z}^{2}-\\frac{24{y}^{2}}{{z}^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9c65c1ea-1534-5429-9fa5-eb19d6c92c34" :answer/id #uuid "9c65c1ea-1534-5429-9fa5-eb19d6c92c34" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-{z}^{6}+6y{z}^{2}-\\frac{6{y}^{2}}{{z}^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-bb756db8-08d4-574e-9721-71fce97c078b" :question/id #uuid "bb756db8-08d4-574e-9721-71fce97c078b" :question/math-academy-id "q-110705" :question/problem "Find the coefficient of ${t}^{9}$ in the expansion of  ${(4-2{t}^{3})}^{4}.$" :question/worked-solution "Since the exponent of the binomial expression is $n=4,$ we see that the exponents of the binomial expansion must follow the pattern\n\n$$\n{({t}^{3})}^{0},\\,{({t}^{3})}^{1},\\,{({t}^{3})}^{2},\\,{({t}^{3})}^{3},\\,{({t}^{3})}^{4}\n$$\n\nOf these, the term that returns ${t}^{9}$ is the fourth. So, we are looking for the fourth term in the binomial expansion.\n\nThe $4$th row in Pascal's Triangle is $(1,4,6,4,1).$ Therefore, the fourth term in the binomial expansion is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4({4}^{1}){(-2{t}^{3})}^{3} & =4(4)(-8{t}^{9})=-128{t}^{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the coefficient of ${t}^{9}$ in the expansion of ${(4-2{t}^{3})}^{4}$ is $-128.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-1e586cdb-56eb-504c-81fd-9a35ab291f47"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-1e586cdb-56eb-504c-81fd-9a35ab291f47" :answer-field/id #uuid "1e586cdb-56eb-504c-81fd-9a35ab291f47" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-06b6d424-6151-5260-8d1a-6a2900f6f79d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-06b6d424-6151-5260-8d1a-6a2900f6f79d" "answer-5e8bd7b4-4ec6-5fb7-b481-83d13b4b0b83" "answer-adf4bf83-0cea-5c2a-b235-8ac4e1021977" "answer-192cab35-768d-5181-8fec-591ea58412f3" "answer-0a4d370c-d16f-5c25-b9ab-d451642a1aed"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-06b6d424-6151-5260-8d1a-6a2900f6f79d" :answer/id #uuid "06b6d424-6151-5260-8d1a-6a2900f6f79d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-128" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5e8bd7b4-4ec6-5fb7-b481-83d13b4b0b83" :answer/id #uuid "5e8bd7b4-4ec6-5fb7-b481-83d13b4b0b83" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-32" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-adf4bf83-0cea-5c2a-b235-8ac4e1021977" :answer/id #uuid "adf4bf83-0cea-5c2a-b235-8ac4e1021977" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-96" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-192cab35-768d-5181-8fec-591ea58412f3" :answer/id #uuid "192cab35-768d-5181-8fec-591ea58412f3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "256" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0a4d370c-d16f-5c25-b9ab-d451642a1aed" :answer/id #uuid "0a4d370c-d16f-5c25-b9ab-d451642a1aed" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "96" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-7cdcec28-9eea-5d7b-863d-1e35767ec2c5" :question/id #uuid "7cdcec28-9eea-5d7b-863d-1e35767ec2c5" :question/math-academy-id "q-110707" :question/problem "Find the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ in the expansion of  $(2x-3)^{4}.$" :question/worked-solution "Since the exponent of the binomial expression is $n=4,$ we see that the exponents of the binomial expansion must follow the pattern\n\n$$\n{x}^{4},\\,{x}^{3},\\,{x}^{2},\\,{x}^{1},\\,{x}^{0}\n$$\n\nOf these, the term that returns ${x}^{2}$ is the third. So, we are looking for the third term in the binomial expansion.\n\nThe $4$th row in Pascal's Triangle is $(1,4,6,4,1).$ Therefore, the third term in the binomial expansion is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6(2x)^{2}{(-3)}^{2} & =6(4{x}^{2})(9)=216{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ in the expansion of $(2x-3)^{4}$ is $216.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-4412a8b1-5a3b-5f0c-ab80-8e1bb5a30756"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-4412a8b1-5a3b-5f0c-ab80-8e1bb5a30756" :answer-field/id #uuid "4412a8b1-5a3b-5f0c-ab80-8e1bb5a30756" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-d0599db5-4e2d-50b8-aca9-30e3737df7e8" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a963df3d-8d44-5507-a2b7-f23e331ccbd4" "answer-e5700f82-0652-5901-842f-672506c08567" "answer-84be836c-a587-5a9f-92a5-4202ac33a7d9" "answer-d0599db5-4e2d-50b8-aca9-30e3737df7e8" "answer-d8c1149e-90ad-53e2-8abd-dc4cfebb4faa"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a963df3d-8d44-5507-a2b7-f23e331ccbd4" :answer/id #uuid "a963df3d-8d44-5507-a2b7-f23e331ccbd4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-36" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e5700f82-0652-5901-842f-672506c08567" :answer/id #uuid "e5700f82-0652-5901-842f-672506c08567" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-72" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-84be836c-a587-5a9f-92a5-4202ac33a7d9" :answer/id #uuid "84be836c-a587-5a9f-92a5-4202ac33a7d9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "128" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d0599db5-4e2d-50b8-aca9-30e3737df7e8" :answer/id #uuid "d0599db5-4e2d-50b8-aca9-30e3737df7e8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "216" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d8c1149e-90ad-53e2-8abd-dc4cfebb4faa" :answer/id #uuid "d8c1149e-90ad-53e2-8abd-dc4cfebb4faa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "72" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-583748e6-2c8b-5634-bc36-5084c91f8d81" :question/id #uuid "583748e6-2c8b-5634-bc36-5084c91f8d81" :question/math-academy-id "q-73162" :question/problem "Find the coefficient of ${x}^{4}$ in the expansion of  ${(z+2x)}^{4}.$" :question/worked-solution "Since the exponent of the binomial expression is $n=4,$ we see that the exponents of the binomial expansion must follow the pattern\n\n$$\n{z}^{4}{x}^{0},\\,{z}^{3}{x}^{1},\\,{z}^{2}{x}^{2},\\,{z}^{1}{x}^{3},\\,{z}^{0}{x}^{4},\n$$\n\nOf these, the term that returns ${x}^{4}$ is the fifth. So, we are looking for the fifth term in the binomial expansion.\n\nThe $4$th row in Pascal's Triangle is $(1,4,6,4,1).$ Therefore, the fifth term in the binomial expansion is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1{(z)}^{0}{(2x)}^{4}=1(1)(16{x}^{4})=16{x}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the coefficient of ${x}^{4}$ in the expansion of ${(z+2x)}^{4}$ is $16.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-8197bfc5-0ad1-5f9c-bf1d-1334bb43fb45"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-8197bfc5-0ad1-5f9c-bf1d-1334bb43fb45" :answer-field/id #uuid "8197bfc5-0ad1-5f9c-bf1d-1334bb43fb45" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-0cc8e8c3-2539-5d2e-b249-962fdc897a63" :answer-field/choices ["answer-0cc8e8c3-2539-5d2e-b249-962fdc897a63" "answer-2aa4b33e-bd16-5df7-a8d1-f47a14078132" "answer-493bcceb-1e87-5ee7-8c50-0ef2a21d1f9d" "answer-1aa04281-3db0-59dc-8d17-70ecc80f8121" "answer-ed0aaff4-ceb1-5c9b-82e1-bd1f4b766029"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0cc8e8c3-2539-5d2e-b249-962fdc897a63" :answer/id #uuid "0cc8e8c3-2539-5d2e-b249-962fdc897a63" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "16" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2aa4b33e-bd16-5df7-a8d1-f47a14078132" :answer/id #uuid "2aa4b33e-bd16-5df7-a8d1-f47a14078132" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "32" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-493bcceb-1e87-5ee7-8c50-0ef2a21d1f9d" :answer/id #uuid "493bcceb-1e87-5ee7-8c50-0ef2a21d1f9d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1aa04281-3db0-59dc-8d17-70ecc80f8121" :answer/id #uuid "1aa04281-3db0-59dc-8d17-70ecc80f8121" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "64" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ed0aaff4-ceb1-5c9b-82e1-bd1f4b766029" :answer/id #uuid "ed0aaff4-ceb1-5c9b-82e1-bd1f4b766029" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-1561558e-21f5-576d-9b7c-91bcf7619901" :question/id #uuid "1561558e-21f5-576d-9b7c-91bcf7619901" :question/math-academy-id "q-12488" :question/problem "Find the coefficient of ${s}^{5}$ in the expansion of  $(3+2s)^{5}.$" :question/worked-solution "Since the exponent of the binomial expression is $n=5,$ we see that the exponents of the binomial expansion must follow the pattern\n\n$$\n{s}^{0},\\,{s}^{1},\\,{s}^{2},\\,{s}^{3},\\,{s}^{4},\\,{s}^{5}.\n$$\n\nOf these, the term that returns ${s}^{5}$ is the fifth. So, we are looking for the fifth term in the binomial expansion.\n\nThe $5$th row in Pascal's Triangle is $(1,5,10,10,5,1).$ Therefore, the fifth term in the binomial expansion is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1(3)^{0}(2s)^{5} & =32{s}^{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the coefficient of ${s}^{5}$ in the expansion of $(3+2s)^{5}$ is $32.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-3df69f96-84ad-5e71-9c31-65c276575d99"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-3df69f96-84ad-5e71-9c31-65c276575d99" :answer-field/id #uuid "3df69f96-84ad-5e71-9c31-65c276575d99" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-82d06102-2f93-5516-8d74-5c64ca9e9586" :answer-field/choices ["answer-61cc7245-8546-56f3-82b8-2f0c2bf433bc" "answer-c14257ed-a13e-50f0-ad3d-677e3731752d" "answer-82d06102-2f93-5516-8d74-5c64ca9e9586" "answer-328b96bc-3327-5e07-b15f-09c688c72d42" "answer-6edfe013-3073-56cc-bc3b-310a4cee6524"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-61cc7245-8546-56f3-82b8-2f0c2bf433bc" :answer/id #uuid "61cc7245-8546-56f3-82b8-2f0c2bf433bc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "16" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c14257ed-a13e-50f0-ad3d-677e3731752d" :answer/id #uuid "c14257ed-a13e-50f0-ad3d-677e3731752d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "24" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-82d06102-2f93-5516-8d74-5c64ca9e9586" :answer/id #uuid "82d06102-2f93-5516-8d74-5c64ca9e9586" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "32" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-328b96bc-3327-5e07-b15f-09c688c72d42" :answer/id #uuid "328b96bc-3327-5e07-b15f-09c688c72d42" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "48" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6edfe013-3073-56cc-bc3b-310a4cee6524" :answer/id #uuid "6edfe013-3073-56cc-bc3b-310a4cee6524" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "54" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186101977 :tutorial/content "One interesting number pattern is **Pascal's triangle**, named after Blaise Pascal, a famous French mathematician.\n\nPascal's triangle is made by rows of numbers that form a triangle.\nEach row has one more number than the row above.\nThe first two rows are made of three $1$s in a triangle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} &  & 1 \\\\  & 1 &  & 1 \\\\ ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's construct the next row.\nThe first and last number in each row is always $1.$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} &  &  & 1 \\\\  &  & 1 &  & 1 \\\\  & 1 &  & ◻ &  & 1 \\\\ ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe other numbers are always greater than $1$ and can be found by adding the two closest numbers located in the previous row.\n\nIn our case, the numbers closest to the unknown number and located in the previous row are ${\\color{blue}1}$ and ${\\color{blue}1}.$\nTherefore, the unknown number is the sum of $\\color{blue}1$ and ${\\color{blue}1}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n{\\large\\square} = {\\color{blue}1} + {\\color{blue}1} = 2\n$$\n\nNow, we have the first three rows of Pascal's triangle.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} &  &  & 1 \\\\  &  & 1 &  & 1 \\\\  & 1 &  & 2 &  & 1 \\\\ ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we continue repeating this process for each row, we will get the following results for the first six rows of Pascal's triangle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} &  &  &  &  &  & 1 \\\\  &  &  &  &  & 1 &  & 1 \\\\  &  &  &  & 1 &  & 2 &  & 1 \\\\  &  &  & 1 &  & 3 &  & 3 &  & 1 \\\\  &  & 1 &  & 4 &  & 6 &  & 4 &  & 1 \\\\  & 1 &  & 5 &  & 10 &  & 10 &  & 5 &  & 1 \\\\ ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186101975 :tutorial/content "Multiplying a binomial by itself $n$ times is known as a **binomial expansion**.\nFor instance, multiplying the binomial $(a + b)$ by itself gives\n\n$$\n(a + b)^2 = a^2 + 2ab + b^2.\n$$\n\nLet's consider the binomial expansion of \n\n$$\n(a+b)^n,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is a positive integer.\n\nTo write out the expansion, we use two patterns to simplify most of the hard work: the **exponent pattern** and the **coefficient pattern**.\n\n- The **exponent pattern** is\n \n\n$$\na^{\\color{red}n}b^{\\color{blue}0},\\quad  a^{\\color{red}{n-1}}b^{{\\color{blue}1}}, \\quad   a^{{\\color{red}n-2}}b^{\\color{blue}2}, \\quad  \\dots\\quad   a^{\\color{red}2}b^{{\\color{blue}n-2}},\\quad    a^{\\color{red}1}b^{{\\color{blue}n - 1}}, \\quad   a^{\\color{red}0}b^{{\\color{blue}n}}.\n$$\n\nThe exponents of $a$ start at $n$ and go down ($n, n-1, \\cdots, 1, 0$) while the exponents of $b$ increase from $0$ to $n.$ In every term, the two exponents always add up to $n.$\n\n- The **coefficient pattern** can be found using Pascal's Triangle:\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} &  &  &  &  &  & 1 \\\\  &  &  &  &  & 1 &  & 1 \\\\  &  &  &  & 1 &  & 2 &  & 1 \\\\  &  &  & 1 &  & 3 &  & 3 &  & 1 \\\\  &  & 1 &  & 4 &  & 6 &  & 4 &  & 1 \\\\  & 1 &  & 5 &  & 10 &  & 10 &  & 5 &  & 1 \\\\ ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe take the top row to be the $0$th row. Then, the $n$th row of Pascal's Triangle are the coefficients of $(a+b)^n.$\n\nLet's use this to expand the binomial \n\n$$\n(a+b)^3.\n$$\n\nThe exponent pattern is\n\n$$\na^{\\color{red}3}b^{\\color{blue}0},\\quad  a^{\\color{red}2}b^{\\color{blue}1}, \\quad a^{\\color{red}1}b^{\\color{blue}2}, \\quad  a^{\\color{red}0}b^{\\color{blue}3}.\n$$\n\nSince $n=3,$ the coefficient pattern is found using the $3$rd row in Pascal's Triangle: $(1,3,3,1).$ \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n(a + b)^3  \n= \\mathbf{1}a^{\\color{red}3}b^{\\color{blue}0} + \\mathbf{3}a^{\\color{red}2}b^{\\color{blue}1} + \\mathbf{3}a^{\\color{red}1}b^{\\color{blue}2} + \\mathbf{1}a^{\\color{red}0}b^{\\color{blue}3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, simplifying the above gives\n\n$$\n(a + b)^3= a^{3} + 3a^{2}b + 3ab^{2} + b^{3} ,\n$$\n\nand we're done!" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186101990 :tutorial/content "The following table shows the first six binomial expansions.\n\n| **Power** | **Pascal's Triangle** | **Binomial** | **Binomial Expansion** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $\\mathbf{1}$ | $(a + b)^0$ | $\\mathbf{1}$ |\n| $1$ | $\\mathbf{1}\\quad\\mathbf{1}$ | $(a + b)^1$ | $\\mathbf{1}a + \\mathbf{1}b$ |\n| $2$ | $\\mathbf{1}\\quad\\mathbf{2}\\quad\\mathbf{1}$ | $(a + b)^2$ | $\\mathbf{1}a^2 + \\mathbf{2}ab + \\mathbf{1}b^2$ |\n| $3$ | $\\mathbf{1}\\quad\\mathbf{3}\\quad\\mathbf{3}\\quad\\mathbf{1}$ | $(a + b)^3$ | $\\mathbf{1}a^3 + \\mathbf{3}a^2b + \\mathbf{3}ab^2 + \\mathbf{1}b^3$ |\n| $4$ | $\\mathbf{1}\\quad\\mathbf{4}\\quad\\mathbf{6}\\quad\\mathbf{4}\\quad\\mathbf{1}$ | $(a + b)^4$ | $\\mathbf{1}a^4 + \\mathbf{4}a^3b + \\mathbf{6}a^2b^2 + \\mathbf{4}ab^3 + \\mathbf{1}b^4$ |\n| $5$ | $\\mathbf{1}\\quad\\!\\mathbf{5}\\quad\\!\\mathbf{10}\\!\\quad\\!\\mathbf{10}\\quad\\!\\mathbf{5}\\quad\\!\\mathbf{1}$ | $(a + b)^5$ | $\\mathbf{1}a^5 \\!+\\! \\mathbf{5}a^4b \\!+\\! \\mathbf{10}a^3b^2 \\!+\\! \\mathbf{10}a^2b^3 \\!+\\! \\mathbf{5}ab^4 \\!+\\! \\mathbf{1}b^5$ |" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186101026 :activity/expected-seconds 900.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186078527 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-85ad9b13-1cf5-585c-ad88-3c1ab1d51635" "question-a93ce581-ceca-53ce-935d-30328bce90e2"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186078142 :question/worked-solution "A rule is a function if and only if *every* input is mapped to *precisely one* output.\n\nIn particular, a rule is *not* a function in the following cases:\n\n- An input value is not assigned to an output value. For example, the following situation is *not allowed* for functions (an input value with no arrow from it):\n\n![](images/bb/bb53865d210d631d78e5ce48aa96ecff99da8995936a085c789c3471ffb9e6a0.png)\n\n \n\n- An input value is assigned to more than one output value. For example, the following situation is *not allowed* for functions (an input value with more than one arrow from it):\n\n![](images/18/18831dc73e561faa69748fed111eb7e1024d35849fb6624594ae08d8c35301ee.png)\n\n \nIn this particular example, the rule *is* a function because *each* input value is assigned *precisely one* output value." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186078528 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-4de85998-baf3-5654-8a93-fb6fc65cf489"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186078381 :question/problem "Which missing $xy$-pair would make the following rule a function?\n\n| **$x$** | $-3$ | $-1$ | ? | $3$ | $5$ | $7$ | $9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $0$ | $4$ | ? | $0$ | $2$ | $1$ | $7$ |\n\nI. $(1,2)$\n\nII. $(7,3)$\n\nIII. $(-1,5)$" :question/worked-solution "A rule is a function if and only if every input is mapped to *precisely one* output.\n\n- If we substitute the $xy$-pair $(1,2)$ into our table, we get a function.\n\n| **$x$** | $-3$ | $-1$ | $\\color{blue}1$ | $3$ | $5$ | $7$ | $9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $0$ | $4$ | $2$ | $0$ | $2$ | $1$ | $7$ |\n\nNotice that all values in the first row are *distinct* (i.e., different). Every input value is assigned to *precisely one* output value.\n\n- None of the other $xy$-pairs gives rules that are functions. So, for example, substituting the $xy$-pair $(-1,5)$ gives a rule that is *not* a function. \n\n| **$x$** | $-3$ | $\\color{red}-1$ | $\\color{red}-1$ | $3$ | $5$ | $7$ | $9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $0$ | $4$ | $5$ | $0$ | $2$ | $1$ | $7$ |\n\nNotice that the first row contains the value $\\color{red}x=-1$ twice. The input value $-1$ is assigned to *more than one* output value ($4$ and $5$)." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-85ad9b13-1cf5-585c-ad88-3c1ab1d51635" :question/id #uuid "85ad9b13-1cf5-585c-ad88-3c1ab1d51635" :question/math-academy-id "q-31702" :question/problem "![](images/d3/d396fe1ef272d88412cb870fc78975517f32dbb3738f4913e34bdf677daee546.png)\n\nIs the above rule a function?" :question/worked-solution "A rule is a function if and only if *every* input is mapped to *precisely one* output.\n\nIn particular, a rule is *not* a function in the following cases:\n\n- An input value is not assigned to an output value. For example, the following situation is *not allowed* for functions (an input value with no arrow from it):\n\n![](images/bb/bb53865d210d631d78e5ce48aa96ecff99da8995936a085c789c3471ffb9e6a0.png)\n\n \n\n- An input value is assigned to more than one output value. For example, the following situation is *not allowed* for functions (an input value with more than one arrow from it):\n\n![](images/18/18831dc73e561faa69748fed111eb7e1024d35849fb6624594ae08d8c35301ee.png)\n\n \n\nIn this particular example, the rule *is* a function because *each* input value is assigned *precisely one* output value." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-498fb75a-0671-58f0-8b7a-c51a0893cf04"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-498fb75a-0671-58f0-8b7a-c51a0893cf04" :answer-field/id #uuid "498fb75a-0671-58f0-8b7a-c51a0893cf04" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-f1d29d2f-e6ed-5d7e-a30e-0d159afac303" :answer-field/choices ["answer-5895e7b6-6a93-519a-b910-a01d6f8be974" "answer-6c24f5fd-0d38-5e73-aeda-5294b9b679ec" "answer-200d6aab-2013-5bd4-9138-56d7d25f5431" "answer-f1d29d2f-e6ed-5d7e-a30e-0d159afac303" "answer-045c6200-a555-54f7-bf4a-80441d1ccdad"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5895e7b6-6a93-519a-b910-a01d6f8be974" :answer/id #uuid "5895e7b6-6a93-519a-b910-a01d6f8be974" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "No, since both sets have different numbers of elements" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6c24f5fd-0d38-5e73-aeda-5294b9b679ec" :answer/id #uuid "6c24f5fd-0d38-5e73-aeda-5294b9b679ec" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "No, since there is at least one output with more than one input" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-200d6aab-2013-5bd4-9138-56d7d25f5431" :answer/id #uuid "200d6aab-2013-5bd4-9138-56d7d25f5431" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Not enough information" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f1d29d2f-e6ed-5d7e-a30e-0d159afac303" :answer/id #uuid "f1d29d2f-e6ed-5d7e-a30e-0d159afac303" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Yes, since each input is assigned to exactly one output" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-045c6200-a555-54f7-bf4a-80441d1ccdad" :answer/id #uuid "045c6200-a555-54f7-bf4a-80441d1ccdad" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Yes, since each output is related to at least one input" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-a93ce581-ceca-53ce-935d-30328bce90e2" :question/id #uuid "a93ce581-ceca-53ce-935d-30328bce90e2" :question/math-academy-id "q-130470" :question/problem "![](images/26/26d5274aedc2f04b31a7c122dd3c23257834936e510f187b018f2394c8b6abd9.png)\n\nIs the above rule a function?" :question/worked-solution "A rule is a function if and only if *every* input is mapped to *precisely one* output.\n\nIn particular, a rule is *not* a function in the following cases:\n\n- An input value is not assigned to an output value. For example, the following situation is *not allowed* for functions (an input value with no arrow from it):\n\n![](images/bb/bb53865d210d631d78e5ce48aa96ecff99da8995936a085c789c3471ffb9e6a0.png)\n\n \n\n- An input value is assigned to more than one output value. For example, the following situation is *not allowed* for functions (an input value with more than one arrow from it):\n\n![](images/18/18831dc73e561faa69748fed111eb7e1024d35849fb6624594ae08d8c35301ee.png)\n\n \n\nIn this particular example, the rule *is* a function because *each* input value is assigned *precisely one* output value." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-aea26438-c3d8-515d-8034-8cb5d6f50bde"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-aea26438-c3d8-515d-8034-8cb5d6f50bde" :answer-field/id #uuid "aea26438-c3d8-515d-8034-8cb5d6f50bde" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-46641fd3-a924-5689-8b29-6b47d95ad36d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-731cb4bc-bbc2-5e95-b79a-9d9f37730208" "answer-afd3f74c-2c2e-5a58-b40f-52c2a8f52363" "answer-3bd68649-b47b-539f-afa8-1d38f4403893" "answer-46641fd3-a924-5689-8b29-6b47d95ad36d" "answer-1d8f78f3-9170-5ee2-a54a-196cfab0a582"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-731cb4bc-bbc2-5e95-b79a-9d9f37730208" :answer/id #uuid "731cb4bc-bbc2-5e95-b79a-9d9f37730208" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "No, since both sets have different numbers of elements" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-afd3f74c-2c2e-5a58-b40f-52c2a8f52363" :answer/id #uuid "afd3f74c-2c2e-5a58-b40f-52c2a8f52363" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "No, since there is at least one output with more than one input" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3bd68649-b47b-539f-afa8-1d38f4403893" :answer/id #uuid "3bd68649-b47b-539f-afa8-1d38f4403893" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Not enough information" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-46641fd3-a924-5689-8b29-6b47d95ad36d" :answer/id #uuid "46641fd3-a924-5689-8b29-6b47d95ad36d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Yes, since each input is assigned to exactly one output" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1d8f78f3-9170-5ee2-a54a-196cfab0a582" :answer/id #uuid "1d8f78f3-9170-5ee2-a54a-196cfab0a582" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Yes, since each output is related to at least one input" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-4de85998-baf3-5654-8a93-fb6fc65cf489" :question/id #uuid "4de85998-baf3-5654-8a93-fb6fc65cf489" :question/math-academy-id "q-31695" :question/problem "Which missing $xy$-pair would make the following rule a function?\n\n| **$x$** | $-8$ | $-5$ | $-2$ | ? | $3$ | $6$ | $7$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $10$ | $7$ | $-1$ | ? | $6$ | $8$ | $15$ |" :question/worked-solution "A rule is a function if and only if every input is mapped to *precisely one* output.\n\n- If we substitute the $xy$-pair $(1,2)$ into our table, we get a function.\n\n| **$x$** | $-8$ | $-5$ | $-2$ | $1$ | $3$ | $6$ | $7$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $10$ | $7$ | $-1$ | $2$ | $6$ | $8$ | $15$ |\n\nNotice that all values in the first row are *distinct* (i.e., different). Every input value is assigned to *precisely one* output value.\n\n- None of the other $xy$-pairs gives rules that are functions. So, for example, substituting the $xy$-pair $(-2,12)$ gives a rule that is *not* a function. \n\n| **$x$** | $-8$ | $-5$ | $-2$ | $-2$ | $3$ | $6$ | $7$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $10$ | $7$ | $-1$ | $12$ | $6$ | $8$ | $15$ |\n\nNotice that the first row contains the value $x=-2$ twice. The input value $-2$ is assigned to *more than one* output value ($-1$ and $12$)." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-27674089-a4c2-5e56-bbd1-5e9f749f8c16"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-27674089-a4c2-5e56-bbd1-5e9f749f8c16" :answer-field/id #uuid "27674089-a4c2-5e56-bbd1-5e9f749f8c16" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-1fc725ed-3feb-5bd6-a8bc-fb71caadc806" :answer-field/choices ["answer-0dbaa9a3-74ca-52a1-b952-f3cf1d7c97b8" "answer-35ecb1c6-c69c-563b-8ca7-792e160663a9" "answer-1fc725ed-3feb-5bd6-a8bc-fb71caadc806" "answer-9ffbc4a0-d6d9-505f-9699-aeab2f70a91b" "answer-e9e28e8b-f4cd-5741-a59a-cfa8f69168a5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0dbaa9a3-74ca-52a1-b952-f3cf1d7c97b8" :answer/id #uuid "0dbaa9a3-74ca-52a1-b952-f3cf1d7c97b8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-2,12)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-35ecb1c6-c69c-563b-8ca7-792e160663a9" :answer/id #uuid "35ecb1c6-c69c-563b-8ca7-792e160663a9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-8,1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1fc725ed-3feb-5bd6-a8bc-fb71caadc806" :answer/id #uuid "1fc725ed-3feb-5bd6-a8bc-fb71caadc806" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(1,2)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9ffbc4a0-d6d9-505f-9699-aeab2f70a91b" :answer/id #uuid "9ffbc4a0-d6d9-505f-9699-aeab2f70a91b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(3,9)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e9e28e8b-f4cd-5741-a59a-cfa8f69168a5" :answer/id #uuid "e9e28e8b-f4cd-5741-a59a-cfa8f69168a5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(7,-2)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186078878 :question/problem "Consider a rule between $x$ and $y$ given by the table below.\n\n| **$x$** | $12$ | $9$ | $7$ | $3$ | $6$ | $9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $15$ | $12$ | $9$ | $6$ | $3$ | $0$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The rule is a function\n\nII. The rule is **not** a function\n\nIII. The rule maps the input $x=15$ to the output $y=12$" :question/worked-solution "A rule is a function if and only if every input is mapped to precisely one output.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement I is false, and statement II is true. \n\n| **$x$** | $12$ | $9$ | $7$ | $3$ | $6$ | $9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $15$ | $12$ | $9$ | $6$ | $3$ | $0$ |\n\nNotice that the first row contains the value $x=9$ twice. The input value $x=9$ is assigned to *more than one* output value ($12$ and $0$).\n\n- Statement III is false. The input $x=15$ is not mapped to anything.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186076046 :answer-field/correct 17592186076051 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186079616 :tutorial/content "A **function** is a rule where *every* input value is assigned *precisely one* output value. \n\nAs an example, let's consider the rule defined by\n\n$$\nf(x) = x+3\n$$\n\nwhere $x$ can be any number from the **set** $\\{1,2,3,4\\}.$ A set is a collection of numbers we can use in our rule. \n\nLet's calculate the possible values of $f(x)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1) & =1+3=4 \\\\ f(2) & =2+3=5 \\\\ f(3) & =3+3=6 \\\\ f(4) & =4+3=7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can represent this rule using a **mapping diagram**, like the one shown below.\n\n![](images/47/4714001592222932934cb7edd5852542a2e990e6680daad85e2db88b9c7cef47.png)\n\nThis rule *is* a function because *every* input is mapped to *precisely one* output." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186079694 :tutorial/content "Now let's consider the rule given by the following mapping diagram:\n\n![](images/b0/b02a511f8043bb7e39df2de082befa267f795948ea6c1cc1ef38eb9df4a8cb1d.png)\n\nThis rule is *not* a function because *at least one* input is assigned to *more than one* output. In this case, the input $x=1$ is mapped to *both* $2$ and $-2.$\n\nFinally, let's consider the rule given by the following mapping diagram:\n\n![](images/be/bea4ee8835a6111e105670863edede9450ad26dbfbea631454d5c8d6cd76a6a1.png)\n\nThis rule is *not* a function because *at least one* input is not assigned to an output. In this case, the input $0$ is not assigned an output." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186079696 :tutorial/content "So, we must remember that a rule is a function if and only if *every* input is mapped to *precisely one* output.\n\nIn particular, a rule is *not* a function in the following cases:\n\n- An input value is not assigned an output value. For example, the following situation is *not allowed* for functions (an input value with no arrow from it):\n\n![](images/bb/bb53865d210d631d78e5ce48aa96ecff99da8995936a085c789c3471ffb9e6a0.png)\n\n \n\n- An input value is assigned to more than one output value. For example, the following situation is *not allowed* for functions (an input value with more than one arrow from it):\n\n![](images/18/18831dc73e561faa69748fed111eb7e1024d35849fb6624594ae08d8c35301ee.png)" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186079695 :tutorial/content "When a rule is represented as a table, we have a function if and only if all values in the *first row* are *distinct* (i.e., different).\n\nLet's consider some examples.\n\nThe rule defined by the following table is a function. Notice that all values in the first row are distinct. Every input value is assigned to exactly one output value.\n\n| **$x$** | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $-2$ | $2$ | $0$ | $7$ | $3$ | $0$ |\n\n \n\nOn the other hand, the rule defined by the following table is *not* a function. Notice that the first row contains the value $\\color{red}3$ twice. The input value $3$ is assigned to two output values ($0$ and $-1$).\n\n| **$x$** | $1$ | $2$ | $\\color{red}3$ | $4$ | $\\color{red}3$ | $6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $-2$ | $2$ | $0$ | $7$ | $-1$ | $5$ |" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186078655 :activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186171291 :answer-field/choices 17592186171296] [:db/retract 17592186171291 :answer-field/choices 17592186171292] [:db/retract 17592186171291 :answer-field/choices 17592186171293] [:db/retract 17592186171291 :answer-field/choices 17592186171294] [:db/retract 17592186171291 :answer-field/choices 17592186171295] [:db/retract 17592186171315 :answer-field/choices 17592186171316] [:db/retract 17592186171315 :answer-field/choices 17592186171317] [:db/retract 17592186171315 :answer-field/choices 17592186171318] [:db/retract 17592186171315 :answer-field/choices 17592186171319] [:db/retract 17592186171315 :answer-field/choices 17592186171320] [:db/retract 17592186172616 :question/answer-fields 17592186171321] {:db/id 17592186172025 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-42d1d2ed-a4fb-5a99-995b-31b25dd2611a"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186171831 :question/problem "![](images/9c/9cba1591f419653a58de73519f06b9c64b57e92b88376a7a3a1a22f62e45bc74.svg)\nConsider the matrix $P$ shown above. Given that $A=PDP^T$ is an orthogonal diagonalization of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ for some diagonal matrix $D,$ which of the following could be the first column of the matrix $P?$\n\n![](images/db/dbe82125589c07f379dc34f725c5052ebf2f50ace729a5294d749bc82e5f796c.svg)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186172027 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-8da40f3d-5d18-5923-96f9-268309e6cbba" "question-71c26421-0b8a-5150-b5ca-ed9ed8ecdd75"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186171833 :question/problem "![](images/71/71ae5f07963d6fc4006ac682fb99d9654957564b0238494aa5d6f2c6ab3a1161.svg)\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D=P^T \\! AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, which of the following could be the first column of the matrix $P?$\n\n![](images/c0/c037ac599b0f26311ef49cea15a50b3c215f5f66af6677cc400470e36ac1dc12.svg)" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PDP^T,\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD=P^TAP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D=P^T \\! AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are $\\lambda_1=-1$ and $\\lambda_2=1.$ So, the first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda_1=-1.$ \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to $\\lambda_1=-1.$ Computing $A-(-1)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+I & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, $x_2$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain $x_1=x_2.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n![](images/e8/e89397c2eb8ac9affa119178536b827fe4c9f710c5569dbdc5138c3d6498a413.svg)\n\nTherefore, setting $x_2=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n![](images/cb/cbbbb9019a6fe09c63e1a69478321553144988117e35d9947336c964841b9cfc.svg)\n\nwhere $\\| \\mathbf{v}_1 \\| = \\sqrt{2}.$ Dividing $\\mathbf{v}_1$ by its norm, we obtain the first column of $P\\mathbin{:}$ \n![](images/ea/ea9a9bf2fc7a7328202006234fa0288237b663ae31c382cc0e98f301eb3999ae.svg)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186172026 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-23817e7f-6a06-51ca-bb89-ae0fdb8c7004"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186171832 :question/problem "![](images/ff/ff3e6c7ad9b4c8aa8e45f374a284c0f55a4655acab5ce77650e5e5eab8a3eea7.svg)\nThe matrix $A$ above has eigenvalues $\\lambda_1=5$ and $\\lambda_2=-8.$ Find a diagonal matrix $D$ and an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $D=P^T \\! A P.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PDP^T,\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD=P^TAP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nNote that $A$ is symmetric. Therefore, it is orthogonally diagonalizable. Let's now go through the process.\n\n**Step 1.**  Notice that we already have the eigenvalues of $A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$\\qquad$ $\\lambda_1=5\\quad$ and $\\quad \\lambda_2=-8$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For $\\lambda_1=5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-5I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -4\\end{aligned}]-5[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -9\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-5I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector ![](images/7e/7e2e5d56fa414820a4a21b54c1c382e822da079044416e35d511f75a8b4a4a3f.svg) Dividing $\\mathbf{v}_1$ by its norm $\\| \\mathbf{v}_1  \\| = \\sqrt{13},$ we get \n![](images/69/69886ad4b83344964ea9341595b201aaf8725d805afc0b71036413708ecbe1bf.svg)\n\n- For $\\lambda_2=-8,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-8)I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -4\\end{aligned}]+8[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-8)I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector ![](images/4d/4d63a240ce9979a10db9b4b339c5e66387f030b7434e6fe544d32b1a122cb94d.svg) Dividing $\\mathbf{v}_2$ by its norm $\\| \\mathbf{v}_2  \\| = \\sqrt{13},$ we get \n![](images/f6/f604278ad5afd886a82142b3511ca042a7a1568c2dabb901ae7fb117508c2953.svg)\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n![](images/54/54e0d9e7d7cd07271dfc6a6c3439a7c394e5bf3d85f0630dd1c6f9889561f2c0.svg)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-42d1d2ed-a4fb-5a99-995b-31b25dd2611a" :question/id #uuid "42d1d2ed-a4fb-5a99-995b-31b25dd2611a" :question/math-academy-id "q-94002" :question/problem "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}\\,∗\\, & -\\frac{12}{13} \\\\ \\,∗\\, & \\frac{5}{13}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $P$ shown above. Given that $A=PD{P}^{T}$ is an orthogonal diagonalization of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ for some diagonal matrix $D,$ which of the following could be the first column of the matrix $P?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix.\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nSince  $A=PD{P}^{T}$ is an orthogonal diagonalization of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A,$ the matrix $P$ must be a $2\\times 2$ orthogonal matrix. This means that \n\n- the first column must be orthogonal to the second column, and\n\n- the norm of the first column must be equal to $1.$\n\nAmong the given options, only the vector $[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{13} \\\\ \\frac{12}{13}\\end{aligned}]$ has both properties." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-bce4507a-33b1-53c5-b18c-af85e4f2af25"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-bce4507a-33b1-53c5-b18c-af85e4f2af25" :answer-field/id #uuid "bce4507a-33b1-53c5-b18c-af85e4f2af25" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-3a90f545-ef2d-5c81-b959-9c7ae8555686" :answer-field/choices ["answer-42bf69b6-3377-5a8b-bad9-ebb2f4eaab76" "answer-085dfb60-f817-52bd-b510-171511c7be34" "answer-9c5af8b1-4121-5aa3-8130-1d5d2852b997" "answer-3b5f2132-d567-563d-a029-6013020ee99b" "answer-3a90f545-ef2d-5c81-b959-9c7ae8555686"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-42bf69b6-3377-5a8b-bad9-ebb2f4eaab76" :answer/id #uuid "42bf69b6-3377-5a8b-bad9-ebb2f4eaab76" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{5}{13} \\\\ \\frac{12}{13}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-085dfb60-f817-52bd-b510-171511c7be34" :answer/id #uuid "085dfb60-f817-52bd-b510-171511c7be34" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9c5af8b1-4121-5aa3-8130-1d5d2852b997" :answer/id #uuid "9c5af8b1-4121-5aa3-8130-1d5d2852b997" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3b5f2132-d567-563d-a029-6013020ee99b" :answer/id #uuid "3b5f2132-d567-563d-a029-6013020ee99b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{12}{13} \\\\ -\\frac{5}{13}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3a90f545-ef2d-5c81-b959-9c7ae8555686" :answer/id #uuid "3a90f545-ef2d-5c81-b959-9c7ae8555686" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{13} \\\\ \\frac{12}{13}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186172611 :question/problem "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\,∗\\, \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\,∗\\,\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $P$ shown above. Given that $A=PD{P}^{T}$ is an orthogonal diagonalization of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ for some diagonal matrix $D,$ which of the following could be the second column of the matrix $P?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix.\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nSince  $A=PD{P}^{T}$ is an orthogonal diagonalization of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A,$ the matrix $P$ must be a $2\\times 2$ orthogonal matrix. This means that \n\n- the second column must be orthogonal to the first column, and\n\n- the norm of the second column must be equal to $1.$\n\nAmong the given options, only the vector $[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}]$ has both properties." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186171291 :answer-field/correct "answer-7ade7dd9-f5fe-54b0-9b8e-f4562b03352e" :answer-field/choices ["answer-1e147f75-020d-5d8c-b2c5-aeaaf906cd68" "answer-c53dca38-f58e-5432-8695-431c6b85b38e" "answer-0db62ee4-2a6e-5aca-99a2-279b4cdde2da" "answer-7ade7dd9-f5fe-54b0-9b8e-f4562b03352e" "answer-c856dfd5-97c0-5fc2-9f6b-8bd13f9b8e61"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1e147f75-020d-5d8c-b2c5-aeaaf906cd68" :answer/id #uuid "1e147f75-020d-5d8c-b2c5-aeaaf906cd68" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c53dca38-f58e-5432-8695-431c6b85b38e" :answer/id #uuid "c53dca38-f58e-5432-8695-431c6b85b38e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0db62ee4-2a6e-5aca-99a2-279b4cdde2da" :answer/id #uuid "0db62ee4-2a6e-5aca-99a2-279b4cdde2da" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7ade7dd9-f5fe-54b0-9b8e-f4562b03352e" :answer/id #uuid "7ade7dd9-f5fe-54b0-9b8e-f4562b03352e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c856dfd5-97c0-5fc2-9f6b-8bd13f9b8e61" :answer/id #uuid "c856dfd5-97c0-5fc2-9f6b-8bd13f9b8e61" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-8da40f3d-5d18-5923-96f9-268309e6cbba" :question/id #uuid "8da40f3d-5d18-5923-96f9-268309e6cbba" :question/math-academy-id "q-94029" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -7\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, which of the following could be the second column of the matrix $P.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D={P}^{T}\\,AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-7.$ So, the second column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=-7.$ \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{2}=-7.$ Computing $A-(-7)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+7I & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}]+7[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 9\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 9 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=3{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{10}.$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm, we obtain the second column of $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3\\sqrt{10}}{10} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b14357fc-2106-5858-9b65-b92159c22d9b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b14357fc-2106-5858-9b65-b92159c22d9b" :answer-field/id #uuid "b14357fc-2106-5858-9b65-b92159c22d9b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-b134fbf7-d5b6-5f41-ab04-2a7e561d26d2" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d3c9344d-89ec-5291-9170-aea2d82d9332" "answer-b82e7bd9-f08e-549b-a5ee-19697ab81504" "answer-1af7ba2c-f995-5cdb-b7ee-cabe8c1cbeac" "answer-b134fbf7-d5b6-5f41-ab04-2a7e561d26d2" "answer-3e19570d-c86d-5aef-8ce6-081272d7624d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d3c9344d-89ec-5291-9170-aea2d82d9332" :answer/id #uuid "d3c9344d-89ec-5291-9170-aea2d82d9332" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b82e7bd9-f08e-549b-a5ee-19697ab81504" :answer/id #uuid "b82e7bd9-f08e-549b-a5ee-19697ab81504" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1af7ba2c-f995-5cdb-b7ee-cabe8c1cbeac" :answer/id #uuid "1af7ba2c-f995-5cdb-b7ee-cabe8c1cbeac" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3\\sqrt{10}}{10} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b134fbf7-d5b6-5f41-ab04-2a7e561d26d2" :answer/id #uuid "b134fbf7-d5b6-5f41-ab04-2a7e561d26d2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3\\sqrt{10}}{10} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3e19570d-c86d-5aef-8ce6-081272d7624d" :answer/id #uuid "3e19570d-c86d-5aef-8ce6-081272d7624d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10} \\\\ -\\frac{3\\sqrt{10}}{10}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186172615 :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, which of the following could be the second column of the matrix $P.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D={P}^{T}\\,AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2.$ So, the second column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2.$ \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2.$ Computing $A-(-2)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+2I & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}]+2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-2{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{5}.$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm, we obtain the second column of $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186171315 :answer-field/correct "answer-4a38eb59-71d6-57ae-b362-18321880abad" :answer-field/choices ["answer-6c66f93f-55cb-5fc9-800c-1394bd01e241" "answer-7873082b-15f9-5724-b785-35989be3f136" "answer-4a38eb59-71d6-57ae-b362-18321880abad" "answer-6b57765b-b3fc-58da-ae16-8958c804d7b6" "answer-1afbd007-f1e6-5e55-8cbe-4bcfee30c365"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6c66f93f-55cb-5fc9-800c-1394bd01e241" :answer/id #uuid "6c66f93f-55cb-5fc9-800c-1394bd01e241" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7873082b-15f9-5724-b785-35989be3f136" :answer/id #uuid "7873082b-15f9-5724-b785-35989be3f136" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4a38eb59-71d6-57ae-b362-18321880abad" :answer/id #uuid "4a38eb59-71d6-57ae-b362-18321880abad" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6b57765b-b3fc-58da-ae16-8958c804d7b6" :answer/id #uuid "6b57765b-b3fc-58da-ae16-8958c804d7b6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1afbd007-f1e6-5e55-8cbe-4bcfee30c365" :answer/id #uuid "1afbd007-f1e6-5e55-8cbe-4bcfee30c365" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-71c26421-0b8a-5150-b5ca-ed9ed8ecdd75" :question/id #uuid "71c26421-0b8a-5150-b5ca-ed9ed8ecdd75" :question/math-academy-id "q-94038" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, which of the following could be the first column of the matrix $P.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D={P}^{T}\\,AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-6$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=4.$ So, the first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=-6.$ \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{1}=-6.$ Computing $A-(-6)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+6I & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 3\\end{aligned}]+6[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 9\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 9 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=3{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{10}.$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm, we obtain the first column of $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3\\sqrt{10}}{10} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f7f366c1-707a-566c-ae94-1cc85de0d171"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f7f366c1-707a-566c-ae94-1cc85de0d171" :answer-field/id #uuid "f7f366c1-707a-566c-ae94-1cc85de0d171" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-fa236c72-29cd-50bc-b16a-514c5b7d0eaa" :answer-field/choices ["answer-94417bd7-eb48-5c39-9406-4f41780ccd10" "answer-349a333f-7c5a-5481-82b0-60cb63d7e507" "answer-6a3fb012-e655-54c0-b54f-afddf4c20499" "answer-fa236c72-29cd-50bc-b16a-514c5b7d0eaa" "answer-c96bbaba-0f0d-5501-965f-1c3ab78bf9d3"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-94417bd7-eb48-5c39-9406-4f41780ccd10" :answer/id #uuid "94417bd7-eb48-5c39-9406-4f41780ccd10" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3\\sqrt{10}}{10} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-349a333f-7c5a-5481-82b0-60cb63d7e507" :answer/id #uuid "349a333f-7c5a-5481-82b0-60cb63d7e507" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6a3fb012-e655-54c0-b54f-afddf4c20499" :answer/id #uuid "6a3fb012-e655-54c0-b54f-afddf4c20499" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fa236c72-29cd-50bc-b16a-514c5b7d0eaa" :answer/id #uuid "fa236c72-29cd-50bc-b16a-514c5b7d0eaa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3\\sqrt{10}}{10} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c96bbaba-0f0d-5501-965f-1c3ab78bf9d3" :answer/id #uuid "c96bbaba-0f0d-5501-965f-1c3ab78bf9d3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10} \\\\ -\\frac{3\\sqrt{10}}{10}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-23817e7f-6a06-51ca-bb89-ae0fdb8c7004" :question/id #uuid "23817e7f-6a06-51ca-bb89-ae0fdb8c7004" :question/math-academy-id "q-94049" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe matrix $A$ above has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1.$ Find a diagonal matrix $D$ and an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nNote that $A$ is symmetric. Therefore, it is orthogonally diagonalizable. Let's now go through the process.\n\n**Step 1.**  Notice that we already have the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=3\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-1$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-3I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-3I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-1)I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]+1[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-1)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-dc785a6c-6e3c-5d7b-a2af-c97237d81ba3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-dc785a6c-6e3c-5d7b-a2af-c97237d81ba3" :answer-field/id #uuid "dc785a6c-6e3c-5d7b-a2af-c97237d81ba3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-dbb57498-4f30-5829-bbc1-a63f82ea49d4" :answer-field/choices ["answer-38e29233-e0dc-59a9-8b71-89547fc06293" "answer-4e8be98c-dbfa-5fb0-9905-3ffacd2e675f" "answer-dbb57498-4f30-5829-bbc1-a63f82ea49d4" "answer-a2cf2940-8c8f-50ee-bc73-4ae0c1be7870" "answer-9cbc487c-5094-5d5f-8c80-f2cbf38f3c53"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-38e29233-e0dc-59a9-8b71-89547fc06293" :answer/id #uuid "38e29233-e0dc-59a9-8b71-89547fc06293" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4e8be98c-dbfa-5fb0-9905-3ffacd2e675f" :answer/id #uuid "4e8be98c-dbfa-5fb0-9905-3ffacd2e675f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3\\sqrt{10}}{10} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10} & \\frac{3\\sqrt{10}}{10}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dbb57498-4f30-5829-bbc1-a63f82ea49d4" :answer/id #uuid "dbb57498-4f30-5829-bbc1-a63f82ea49d4" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a2cf2940-8c8f-50ee-bc73-4ae0c1be7870" :answer/id #uuid "a2cf2940-8c8f-50ee-bc73-4ae0c1be7870" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5} & -\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} & \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9cbc487c-5094-5d5f-8c80-f2cbf38f3c53" :answer/id #uuid "9cbc487c-5094-5d5f-8c80-f2cbf38f3c53" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186173154 :tutorial/content "A square matrix $A$ is **orthogonally diagonalizable** if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PDP^T,\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Note that this is equivalent to the equation\n\n$$\nD = P^TAP.\n$$\n\nSo, to orthogonally diagonalize a matrix $A,$ we need to find a diagonal matrix $D$ and an orthogonal matrix $P$ that satisfies the above equations.\n\nA helpful theorem allows us to determine whether a real matrix is orthogonally diagonalizable. It states the following:\n\n*A matrix $A$ with real entries is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.*\n\nConsequently, *every* symmetric matrix is orthogonally diagonalizable!\n\nFor example, the symmetric matrix \n\n![](images/b3/b3970eb885720ce2aa4394918c6c5f9f343e26aa60039df219abf56398f096db.svg)\n\nhas the orthogonal diagonalization\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{P}{{[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]}^{T}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{T}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $P$ is orthogonal, and $D$ is diagonal. As usual, the entries on the main diagonal of $D$ are the eigenvalues of $A.$ Also, the columns of $P$ are *orthonormal* eigenvectors corresponding to the eigenvalues of $A.$\n\nThe procedure of the orthogonal diagonalization of a $2 \\times 2$ real symmetric matrix $A$ is almost the same as the diagonalization of a matrix:\n\n- **Step 1**: Find the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$.\n\n- **Step 2**: Find a basis of  $\\mathbb{R}^2$ consisting of *unit orthogonal* eigenvectors of $A.$\n\n- **Step 3**: Construct the matrices $D$ and $P.$\n\nIn the case of symmetric matrices, the two eigenvectors we compute in step 2 will always be orthogonal, provided that they correspond to different eigenvalues. However, we must also ensure that we *normalize* those eigenvectors." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186172166 :activity/expected-seconds 660.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
